// ff8_accessibility.h - Core header for FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod
//
// Lean post-v0.15.12.0 cleanup. The inline-changelog chain that had
// accreted on the macro line (421 KB by v0.15.11.0) was moved to
// `ff8_accessibility_history.h`, which is NOT included by the build.
// Going forward this header holds only the version macro and the
// system includes other modules rely on it to transitively provide.
//
// The CANONICAL changelog lives in `CHANGELOG.md` at the project root.
// Older entries (pre-v0.15.12.0) are preserved in `CHANGELOG_HISTORY.md`.
//
// FF8 runtime address resolution: see `ff8_addresses.h` /
// `ff8_addresses.cpp` for the resolver that computes addresses at
// runtime using the same offset-chain technique as FFNx.

#pragma once

#include <windows.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

// ================================================================
// FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod version
// Increment on every build change. Must match the top `## vX.Y.Z`
// heading in CHANGELOG.md or `Utilities/push_to_github.ps1` will
// refuse to push.
// ================================================================
#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.18.3.234"  // v0.18.3.115: #70 -- STEEPNESS FILTER replaces the .114 depth gate. The .114 gate (A* refuses triangles below floor-z -1050) was too blunt: Dollet is coastal and its approach dips below -1050 too, so blocking the box canyon also disconnected Dollet -- the .114 BAT logged "navmesh A* NO path" on re-plan and fell back to the coarse fine-grid (wedged 15km on a false-walkable cliff the 1024u grid can't see). Root fix: exclude cliff-FACE triangles from the navmesh at FEED time. floorZ is the MEAN of a triangle's 3 corner z's, so a sheer wall reads as one steep tri whose mean sits mid-drop (depth/floor-step blind to it); the within-triangle z-SPREAD (max corner - min corner) reveals it. Navmesh_AddTriangle now skips any triangle with spread > NM_STEEP_SPREAD (400): box-canyon walls + false-coast cliffs go, gentle terr-29 ramps + the Dollet approach (small spread) stay. A* is ungated again (depth gate retired; s_nmAStarFloorMin left dormant = INT32_MIN). VALIDATION: the [NAVMESH] connectivity diag fires at world-map LOAD (before driving) -- check Dollet says REACHABLE (filter kept it) and the tri count dropped from 157416 (cliffs removed); if Dollet is "not reachable" the threshold clipped the approach -> RAISE NM_STEEP_SPREAD (e.g. 550). Then the [NAVPATH] route should stay shallow (no -1665 dip) and the executor should follow it past 15km. BAT .115: REBUILD via deploy.vbs, confirm Version 0.18.3.115, LOAD the world map (send the [NAVMESH] connectivity line) then auto-drive Dollet on foot; send Logs/ff8_world.log. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. // v0.18.3.114: #70 FIX -- block the DEEP RAVINE in the navmesh drive. The .113 [NAVPATH] dump proved the ungated A* routes the on-foot Dollet drive DOWN into a box canyon (floor -1087..-1665, ~900-1400u below the start -731 / Dollet -264 band) and climbs out the far side -- a route the engine refuses on foot, so Squall wedges on the canyon's east rim ~16km out. floorZ is the MEAN of a triangle's 3 corner z's, so a sheer wall reads as one steep tri whose mean sits mid-drop: the descent steps are gentle (158..235u) and OVERLAP the gentle corridor's validated 176u bottleneck, so a floor-STEP gate can't separate them. The canyon's DEPTH is unambiguous, though. Add s_nmAStarFloorMin (navmesh.inl): A* skips any triangle with floor-z below it; PlanDrivePathNavmesh sets it to -1050 before the A* call, so the search can't enter the ravine (floor <= -1087) and must take the gentle terr-29/road corridor (floor >= ~-731) to Dollet. Search-time gate only -- the validated .107 mesh construction is unchanged; default INT32_MIN keeps the Initialize connectivity diag ungated. [NAVPATH] dump (40 tris + dZ) KEPT to verify the new route stays above -1050; if the gate over-disconnects (logs "navmesh A* NO path") lower toward -1300. BAT .114: REBUILD via deploy.vbs, confirm Version 0.18.3.114, then on foot from the Galbadia save auto-drive Dollet; send Logs/ff8_world.log. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. // v0.18.3.113: #70 DIAGNOSTIC (no behavior change) -- EXTEND the [NAVPATH] dump from 14 to 40 triangles + a per-step dZ (floor-z delta) column, because the .112 dump only captured the GENTLE approach (centroids 0-13, floorZ -731..-937, max single step 235u = the part Squall drives) and stopped SHORT of the box-canyon CLIFF where he wedges at centroid ~16-20 (game ~(-29201,-30115), past the dumped Y=-28841). The dZ column makes the cliff step obvious: ONE big dZ (e.g. a +700u canyon-floor->plateau jump) => gate A* on floor-step at a value below the cliff but above 235u; MANY small dZ (<=235u up a tessellated face) => floor-step gating cannot separate cliff from corridor, exclude high-steepness tris at FEED time instead. STILL no behavior change. REMOVE the [NAVPATH] block after diagnosis. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. BAT .113: REBUILD via deploy.vbs (the .112 build at 19:19 predates this dump-extension -- the last log still shows the 14-entry dump), then on foot from the Galbadia save auto-drive Dollet; send Logs/ff8_world.log -- the [NAVPATH] block (now 40 tris + dZ, logged right before "[PLAN] navmesh centroids") triangles ~13-22 are the goal. // v0.18.3.112: #70 DIAGNOSTIC (no behavior change) -- a [NAVPATH] dump in PlanDrivePathNavmesh logs the A* TRIANGLE path's centroid game(x,y) + floor-z (first 14 tris + goal), to settle whether the navmesh routes the player UP the box-canyon CLIFF (floorZ climbs going into the canyon) or thinks there is a gentle ramp the engine blocks. .111 (centroids) wedged at the SAME box canyon as .110 (funnel): the full drive log shows the centroid path leads the player SOUTH 6 waypoints (19.5km->16km, idx 0->6) then turns NW (steer (-30208,-31232)/(-31232,-31232)) INTO the cliff while Dollet is NE/SE -- so the A* PATH ITSELF routes into the canyon, not the funnel/centroid smoother. The ungated A* (gate=-1, .107) can climb cliffs via steep SHARED-EDGE triangles (the z-aware construction only excludes cliff BRIDGE/PROX links, NOT the cliff-face mesh). The dump's floor-z profile picks the fix (gate A* on floor-step, or exclude high-steepness tris at feed time, gate informed by the actual cliff step). REMOVE the [NAVPATH] block after diagnosis. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. BAT .112: REBUILD (deploy.vbs -- the prior BAT ran the pre-[NAVPATH] .111 DLL, so the log has NO [NAVPATH] lines) then on foot from the Galbadia save auto-drive Dollet; send Logs/ff8_world.log -- the [NAVPATH] block (logged at each plan, right before "[PLAN] navmesh centroids") is the goal. // v0.18.3.111: #70 REVERT the .108 funnel string-pull -> feed the raw A* triangle CENTROIDS as drive waypoints. The F11 at the 17km wedge (a readable 146KB shot) shows Squall steered STRAIGHT INTO a box-canyon CLIFF FACE, steer target locked NW (-31232,-31232) across the rock while Dollet is NE; .109 (clamp OFF) == .110 (clamp ON) byte-for-byte, proving the LOS clamp is IRRELEVANT here -- the FUNNEL routes the chord across the cliff. ROOT (world_map_navmesh.inl Navmesh_FunnelPath): the SSF's DEGENERATE centroid portals at the bridge/proximity links (which share no edge) give the string-pull no real left/right walls, so it pulls taut THROUGH non-walkable terrain. The A* TRIANGLE path is correct (validated .107 to the digit); only the string-pull layered on top is unsound. PlanDrivePathNavmesh now feeds the raw A* centroids (Navmesh_AStar + Navmesh_TriangleCentroidGame, the .106/.107 primitive) rasterized dense as before; each centroid sits inside a walkable triangle and consecutive centroids are a shared edge or a short z-gated neck apart, so chords are too short to span a cliff. Keeps the .110 LOS clamp ACTIVE + the .109 (A) reorder (navmesh before the IsGoalSegment shortcut); Navmesh_FunnelPath left in place but unused. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 reverts to .99. BAT .111: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden; EXPECT a [PLAN] navmesh centroids line (REPLACES navmesh funnel) and the drive to get PAST the 17km cliff-cut wedge -- ideally back to the .107 ~8-12km range or closer, with NO steer target locked WEST/NW across a cliff. If it then wedges ~8-12km at a cliff, that is the executor far-lookahead cutting a corner the coarse 1024u clamp can't see -> the next step is a navmesh-resolution walkability clamp (rasterize navmesh-walkable cells into a fine overlay the executor LOS clamp reads instead of s_walkClassFine). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.110: #70 REVERT the .109 executor coarse-grid bypass -- it REGRESSED (the funnel's dense/degenerate corners NEED the executor LOS clamp to keep the steer target on a clear line; bypassing it locked the steer onto a WRONG-WAY funnel corner -- steer(-31232,-31232) = WEST, away from Dollet -- and the drive walked into a wall at 15-17km, vs .108's clean 4km WITH the clamp). s_driveNavmeshPath now stays FALSE so FineLineClearFootCar + the #83 fwd-guard RUN on the navmesh path (= the .108 executor that drove to 4km). KEEP the .109 (A) reorder (PlanDrivePathNavmesh runs BEFORE the IsGoalSegment shortcut, so the navmesh -- not an empty path -- routes the final approach). LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 reverts to .99. BAT .110: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden; EXPECT the general drive to return to ~4km (NOT the 15-17km WEST wrong-way wedge) and the final leg to attempt the navmesh route (a [PLAN] navmesh funnel line on the final leg). If it now drives to ~4km then wedges NEAR Dollet, that is the LOS clamp's .81-box over-clamp on the final-approach navmesh path -- the narrower next step (retire/exempt the .81 Dollet-coast box cols 104-111 rows 59-69, since the navmesh validates that coast walkable). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.109: #70 EXECUTOR coarse-grid BYPASS on the navmesh path (LOCAL, NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed). The .108 funnel ran (87 tris -> 30 corners -> 30 cells) but STILL wedged 12km from Dollet on OPEN GRASS in the road/railroad canyon corridor (F11 confirmed; NOT a wall), idx pinned 0/30, steer target pinned ~1 cell ahead at (-24064,-30208), player oscillating. ROOT (read the executor steer block): the navmesh route legitimately runs SE through fine cells the COARSE 1024u grid marks blocked (the .81 Dollet-coast box cols 104-111 rows 59-69; the player is at fine(104,66), INSIDE it), and the executor's LOS clamp (FineLineClearFootCar over s_walkClassFine/s_steepFine) walked the steer target all the way back to the player's own cell -- so the cursor never advanced (cell[1] is across the coarse-blocked box, the player never reaches it). The navmesh said walkable; the coarse grid said blocked; the executor trusted the coarse grid. This is the .106 'stage 2': the navmesh routing swap is only complete once the EXECUTOR stops consulting the coarse fine grid. FIX: new s_driveNavmeshPath flag (state.inl; set true in PlanDrivePathNavmesh, cleared at the top of PlanDrivePath) gates OFF both coarse-grid executor checks on the navmesh path -- the FineLineClearFootCar LOS clamp AND the #83 forward-collision guard (both drive.inl). The funnel corridor is navmesh-walkable by construction and the corner-cap keeps the aim inside it, so the clamp is redundant AND harmful there. Now the steer target follows the validated corridor (the corner-capped ~2400u lookahead), the player walks the .81-box cells the engine actually allows, the cursor advances, ARRIVE. The fine-grid path keeps the clamp+guard (flag stays false) -- ZERO change off-navmesh; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 still instant-reverts to .99. BAT .109: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive to Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden -- all three ARRIVE = #70 acceptance + close. EXPECT [YAWDRIVE] steer target to ADVANCE along the route (not pin at (-24064,-30208)) and idx to CLIMB 0 -> 30, with NO wedge. If he now PROGRESSES but crawls/wiggles, the 30 dense corners (the funnel's degenerate bridge/prox-link centroid portals defeat string-pulling) need real-portal computation next -- a smoothness refinement, separate from this wedge. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.108: #68/#70 FUNNEL -- replace the navmesh centroid waypoints with a string-pulled funnel (Simple Stupid Funnel Algorithm) over the A* triangle corridor's PORTALS, so the #68 executor's steer target can no longer aim across walls the winding route goes AROUND (the .107 centroid path wedged the Dollet drive at (-24150,-13185), idx pinned 0/46, oscillating into a sub-cell wall the coarse 1024u LOS clamp missed). New Navmesh_FunnelPath (world_map_navmesh.inl): same ungated A* -> a PORTAL per consecutive tri pair (the shared EDGE where 2 vertices match, oriented L/R by the centroid-travel cross-product; a DEGENERATE portal at the next triangle's centroid for T-junction-bridge / proximity links that share no edge) -> SSF string-pull -> corner points in game coords; PlanDrivePathNavmesh rasterizes the corner polyline into DENSE fine cells (~512u steps, shortest-torus delta, dedup) into s_drivePath, which the #68 executor consumes UNCHANGED (its staircase-aware corner-cap reads each leg as a sustained run and turns at each funnel corner; legs stay inside the walkable corridor by construction so LOS is always clear). Helpers NmTriArea2 + NmSharedVerts, host-compilable under NAVMESH_DIAG. [.107 BAT'd: the z-aware navmesh VALIDATED in-game TO THE DIGIT -- [NAVMESH] 157416 tris, flood 35531 reachable (== offline), Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling/Winhill/Tomb REACHABLE + Balamb NOT, and the gentle corridor PHYSICALLY WALKS (Dollet drive followed the route ~8km, Timber to 1-2km) -- so bit7-overturn + z-aware bridging is CORRECT; the remaining blocker is purely the #68 EXECUTOR.] ONE orientation assumption (documented one-line flip in Navmesh_FunnelPath): the shared vertex LEFT of travel is the funnel's left; if a BAT shows the path hugging the WRONG wall, flip the single `> 0`. Traced offline on a straight corridor -> [start,goal], no spurious corners. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed; flip NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 to revert to .99. BAT .108: Galbadia save -> on-foot auto-drive to Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden -- all three ARRIVE = #70 acceptance + close; EXPECT a [PLAN] 'navmesh funnel: start tri#.. -> goal tri#.. = N tris -> M corners -> K fine cells' line (REPLACES 'navmesh route') and [YAWDRIVE] idx to climb the legs with NO wedge at (-24150,-13185). If Timber still doesn't ENTER once the wedge is fixed, that is a separate field-entry/arrival item. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.107: #70 ROUTING SWAP stage 2 -- OVERTURN the .105 bit7 walkability filter; model on-foot walkability by ELEVATION STEP instead (LOCAL, behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed). BAT .106 confirmed the routing swap WORKS (navmesh route engaged, char followed it ~1.7km closer) but wedged at a sheer cliff 13.3km from Dollet (F11 confirmed). Offline root-cause (reproduced the in-game route to the digit vs the real wmx mesh): the .105 navmesh reaches Dollet ONLY by climbing cliffs -- bottleneck 1018u, and ANY elevation-consistency requirement isolates Dollet (its genuine shared-edge island is 15 tris, the start's 110, DIFFERENT components). The bit7 filter was the WRONG discriminator: it KEEPS the bit7-set cliff/ledge faces (terr 14/15) and DELETES the gentle terr-27/29 ramps (bit7-clear) that connect elevations along the real canyon approach. In the UNFILTERED mesh a gentle corridor to Dollet EXISTS (bottleneck 176u; uses grass/ledge/road/railroad + 13 gentle terr-29 mountain ramps). THREE coupled changes (all offline-validated): (1) segments.inl feeds ALL non-ocean triangles (drop the poly[0x0E]&0x80 skip, back to the .104 feed); (2) navmesh.inl T-junction bridging is now Z-AWARE -- store boundary-edge endpoint z, interpolate across the overlap, bridge only when both ends match within NM_BRIDGE_ZTOL=100 (a cliff top/base share an x,y footprint but NOT z, so the .105 z-ignoring bridge falsely stitched them and let A* climb cliffs); (3) navmesh.inl proximity links are Z-GATED -- connect centroids only within NM_STEP_LINK(400u) horizontally AND NM_LINK_ZSTEP(300u) floor step. A* stays ungated (-1) -- the z-aware construction's max edge step is ~374u, so cliffs are already excluded. This is the fine grid's proven steepness rule (gentle ramps walkable, steep faces blocked) applied to the navmesh. Offline final-validation (unfiltered + z-aware bridge 100 + z-gated prox 300): start component 35531/157416 (23%, one continent); Dollet bottleneck 176, Timber 115, Galbadia Garden 115, Deling 115 -- all gentle, NO cliffs; Balamb Town + Balamb Garden correctly NOT reachable (across ocean). #68 executor + .81/.85/#69 fine-grid stack UNTOUCHED; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 still instant-reverts to the .99 drive. ONE risk to confirm in-BAT: whether the engine physically lets the char walk the gentle terr-29/terr-27 ramps -- strong prior YES (the .99 fine-grid chain, steepness-based, walked most of this corridor; the fine grid relies on gentle-mountain being walkable on other continents). BAT .107: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive to Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden (all three ARRIVE = acceptance test). EXPECT [NAVMESH] '~157416 triangles' (UP from 77447 -- proof the bit7 filter is gone) + a much larger reachable count, Dollet/Timber/G-Garden/Deling REACHABLE + Balamb NOT, then a [PLAN] 'navmesh route' line whose route follows the gentle canyon (no cliff climb) and ARRIVES. If it still wedges, send Logs/ff8_world.log + an F11: the wedge terrain tells us whether a gentle ramp is engine-blocked (-> incorporate poly[0x0F] or lower the ramp gate) or it is the #68 executor end-game. Flip NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 to restore .99. LOCAL-ONLY, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.106: #70 ROUTING SWAP stage 1 (LOCAL, behind NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed). BAT .105 proved the walkability-filtered navmesh's connectivity in-game (77447 tris; Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden REACHABLE, Balamb not), so wire it into the planner. PlanDrivePath now routes on the navmesh FIRST: Navmesh_FindTriangleGame(start)+(dest) -> Navmesh_AStar(start,goal,-1) (ungated; poly[0x0E] walkability IS the passability test) -> convert each triangle CENTROID back to game coords (new Navmesh_TriangleCentroidGame, inverse of NmGameToMeshX/Y) -> fine cell (WorldXToFineCol/Row + PackSeg, dedup consecutive) -> s_drivePath, so the existing #68 yaw executor consumes it UNCHANGED (it already follows s_drivePath as packed fine cells). Falls back to the fine-grid clearance Dijkstra if the navmesh has no path (e.g. mesh not yet built). New NAVMESH_ROUTING toggle (state.inl): 1 = route on the navmesh, 0 = INSTANT revert to the proven .99 fine-grid chain. DELIBERATELY MINIMAL/REVERSIBLE -- the #68 executor, the .81 coast box, the .85 road override, and the #69 height-step guard are ALL UNTOUCHED this stage. The navmesh route is all-walkable so it inherently avoids the false coast/mountains, BUT the executor's #83 forward-collision guard still probes the coarse fine grid: if a walkable navmesh-route cell reads as steep-mountain/.81-blocked there (e.g. the Dollet cliff approach terr14/15), the guard may balk -- that is the EXPECTED stage-1 SIGNAL. If the drive follows the navmesh route to arrival, great; if it wedges, the [YAWDRIVE]/guard trace shows WHERE, and stage 2 retires the fine-grid stack so the fine grid agrees with the navmesh. BAT .106: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive to Dollet (then Timber, then Galbadia Garden). EXPECT a [PLAN] 'navmesh route: A* start tri#.. -> goal tri#.. = N tris -> M fine-cell waypoints' line (NOT '[PLAN] Fine path'), [YAWDRIVE] idx to CLIMB along the waypoints, and -- if the forward-guard doesn't fight a cliff cell -- ARRIVAL with NO false-coast wedge. If it wedges, send Logs/ff8_world.log: the wedge location names the fine-grid blocker to retire in stage 2. Flip NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 to instantly restore the .99 drive. LOCAL-ONLY, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.105: #70 -- WALKABILITY-FILTERED navmesh + step-distance proximity links + ungated flood (LOCAL diag behind NAVMESH_DIAG; NOT pushed). Recovered the engine's on-foot walkability rule from FF8_EN.exe (move-validator 0x54B860 -> selector sub_53E730): poly[0x0E] bit7 is the per-polygon on-foot passability flag (the const-0x32 check), gated by toggle [0x00C75CF4] which inits 1 and is NEVER cleared (only writer 0x542C0C sets 1), so the check is ALWAYS active on the world map. Offline vs world.fs it tracks terrain almost perfectly (mountain 29 / railroad 27 / rough = bit CLEAR = not-walkable; grass/cliff/ledge/road = bit SET). This IS the Dollet 'false coast': the old navmesh only filtered ocean, so the planner routed the character UP mountains the engine refuses on foot and they wedged at the base -- Finding 4 ('collision not in the mesh') is OVERTURNED, the collision WAS in the mesh as E.bit7-clear mountain polys. THREE coupled changes, all validated offline against the real world.fs (see NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT 'WALKABILITY-FLAG TRACK'): (1) LoadTerrainGrid (segments.inl) skips feeding non-walkable polys (poly[WMX_TERRAIN_OFFSET+1] & 0x80 == 0) into the navmesh, mirroring the ocean skip; (2) Navmesh_Build (navmesh.inl) adds step-distance proximity links -- connect walkable triangles whose centroids are within NM_STEP_LINK(400u ~= 2 engine steps) via a 512u bucket grid, because the engine validates the DESTINATION polygon of each ~190u step so the character HOPS across sub-edge-width walkable necks (at the Dollet approach the corridor pinches through mountain slivers: the choke holds 4 walkable + 11 mountain tris, so exact-edge adjacency strands Dollet as a 15-triangle island; general collinear bridging adds only 12 bridges and does NOT fix it -- proximity links do); (3) Navmesh_LogConnectivity floods UNGATED -- the ground-truth walkability filter replaces the NM_CLIMB_STEP height-step heuristic, which wrongly blocked the genuinely-walkable Dollet cliff approach (terr14/15, z spread ~440 > the 400 gate). Offline result, ungated flood from the Galbadia save coord: Dollet + Timber REACHABLE, 20620 of 77447 walkable tris reached (27%, one continent's worth), and Balamb correctly NOT reachable (the 400u links do not bridge the ocean). NON-INVASIVE: the planner/executor are still the .99 chain; nothing routes on the navmesh yet -- this BAT only RE-VERIFIES connectivity in-game. BAT .105: load a Galbadia world-map save (the build fires at terrain load, the connectivity diag at Initialize); EXPECT in Logs/ff8_world.log '[NAVMESH] built ~77447 triangles' (DOWN from 157416 -- proof the walkability filter took effect at the right byte), the flood line '-> ~20620 reachable (walkability-filtered, ungated)', and Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling City REACHABLE with Balamb NOT reachable. If the triangle count does NOT drop from 157416 the poly[0x0E] offset is wrong; if Dollet is still not reachable raise NM_STEP_LINK; if Balamb becomes reachable the proximity radius is bridging water (lower it). If the connectivity matches offline, the NEXT BAT swaps routing onto the navmesh and retires the coarse-grid + .81/.85 compensating stack. LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.104: PORT the container-validated triangle navmesh (new src/world_map_navmesh.inl) into the build as a NON-INVASIVE diagnostic -- build + connectivity probe ONLY, ZERO change to the working planner/executor (the .99 chain still drives). All gated behind NAVMESH_DIAG (LOCAL-only, like WM_RUNTIME_WALK_DIAG). The module was developed offline against the real wmx.obj then host-compiled + run in-container (tests/test_navmesh.cpp, g++ -std=c++17, 0 failures) reproducing the offline numbers TO THE DIGIT: 157416 navigable triangles, 253 connected components after axis-aligned T-junction bridging, largest 74308; flood from the Galbadia save coord (-29270,-24056) at gate 400 reaches Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling City and correctly NOT Balamb (across the ocean); A* Galbadia->Dollet = 30259 units / 126 tris. Integration: LoadTerrainGrid (segments.inl) now (gated) Navmesh_Reset()s before its segment loop, Navmesh_AddTriangle()s each non-ocean triangle from INSIDE the existing RasterizeTriFine block (reusing the SAME verts + i0/i1/i2<vertCount guard, so the navmesh sees triangles identical to the fine grid), then Navmesh_Build()s (vertex dedup -> exact shared-edge adjacency -> T-junction bridging -> CSR) and logs [NAVMESH] tris/components/largest; world_map.cpp gains <vector> + the navmesh.inl include (after geometry.inl, before segments.inl) and Initialize() calls Navmesh_LogConnectivity() from the Galbadia coord. FIRST half of the navmesh plan (NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md): prove the mesh BUILDS + CONNECTS in-game before any routing depends on it. CAVEAT (Finding 4) STANDS: the navmesh canNOT model the Dollet false-coast (engine collision, not in the mesh) -- the .81 exclusion stays; the durable fix is the disassembly track. BAT .104: drive on the world map OR just load a Galbadia save (build fires at terrain load, connectivity diag at Initialize); EXPECT in Logs/ff8_world.log '[NAVMESH] built 157416 triangles, 253 components, largest=74308', the flood line '-> 35527 reachable (gate=400)', Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling City REACHABLE + Balamb not reachable, and '[NAVMESH] A* ref->Dollet: len=30259 tris=126 (path found)'. If the numbers MATCH offline the build is sound in-game and the NEXT BAT swaps routing onto it (retire the coarse fine-grid + .85 override; keep .81); if it CRASHES or the numbers DIFFER the in-game wmx parse diverged -- compare it. LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.103: RTWALK gate relaxation -- the .102 re-gate correctly suppressed the premature entry-tick dump (at entry foot=(0,0) + adj0/adj1=0xFFFFxxxx -> gate refused, no garbage dump) BUT then never fired at all: the adj-sanity requirement (adj0/adj1 in [0x00010000,0x7FFF0000)) never passed because the descriptor dwords stayed 0xFFFF914E/0xFFFFB006, AND the 1200-tick fire-anyway budget (~20s) wasn't reached in the ~15s session window -- so we still have NO post-load probe, which is the entire point. Over-corrected: the adj-sanity should have been a LOGGED OBSERVATION, not a HARD GATE (gating on it blocks the dump in exactly the case -- structural model wrong -- we most need to see). FIX (world_map.cpp Poll): drop adj0ok/adj1ok from the `ready` condition so the dump fires on scene-init alone (descBase!=0 AND foot position valid AND 30-tick settle), ~0.5s after the world map initializes; adj0ok/adj1ok are still computed and surfaced in the firing log as (ptr?0/1). This finally yields the post-load DumpRuntimeWalkability() probe of poly[0..9] + polyCount. EMERGING SIGNAL to resolve next BAT: descBase+0/descBase+12 read 0xFFFF914E/0xFFFFB006 and (since .102 `ready` never fired across ~15s past the settle) appear NOT to resolve to pointers even post-load -- so the descriptor-array model (descBase+N*12 -> adj-list pointer) may be WRONG. BAT .103: drive on the world map; EXPECT '[RTWALK] firing dump (mesh-ready)' ~0.5s after entry, then the poly[0..9] probe -- sane neighbour indices + WALL/END sentinels => model right, was pure timing; still 0xFFFFxxxx with polyCount now 473193 => descriptor model wrong, pivot to re-derive the structure (try the +0 field as a base-relative or signed offset, or re-check what 0x020402DC actually points at). LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.102: RTWALK runtime-walkmesh diagnostic RE-GATE -- fire the dump once the mesh is POPULATED, not merely when [0x020402DC]!=0. The .101 BAT got the first-ever [RTWALK] dump to fire (descBase=0x01E9FDCC, SEH guards caught the bad reads, plumbing proven) but it fired at world-map ENTRY before world.fs streamed the mesh: player pos still (0,0), polyCount(obs)=11, descriptor entries garbage (poly[0] adjPtr=0xFFFF914E; polys 4-9 = small increasing offsets 0x268..0x484, all FAULT). descBase is set during the data-load pointer setup BEFORE the entries it points to are filled in (which coincides with position becoming valid). FIX (world_map.cpp Poll + state.inl): keep polling, only fire once descBase!=0 AND foot position valid (footX|footY) for a 30-tick settle AND the first two descriptor adj-ptrs read as plausible pointers in [0x00010000,0x7FFF0000) (rejects both the 0x2xx offsets and the 0xFFFFxxxx garbage); bounded fire-anyway budget (1200 ticks) still dumps if it never settles -- and a post-load dump that is STILL garbage would mean the structural model, not the timing, is wrong. New s_rtWalkSettleTicks/s_rtWalkPollTicks replace the vestigial countdown, reset on entry. LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Full detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.101: Runtime walkability diag #68 executor CORNER-CAP (the .99 planner fix WORKED -- this is the next, separate blocker). The .99 BAT was a milestone: [PLAN] flipped from 'start already the closest reachable cell (expanded 5) -- direct steer' to a real 'Fine path: 61 cells ... dist=0, expanded 733', and [YAWDRIVE] idx CLIMBED 0->5 as he FOLLOWED it ~6km out of the Dollet shelf (dist 36019->34322). The pocket is SOLVED. He then re-wedged at idx 5/61 -- NOT the pocket, the #68 executor end-game. ROOT: the route is an L (a long WEST leg idx 0-4 at Y=-41472, then a 1-cell-wide NORTH canyon idx 5+ at X=-23040). The steer-target picker scans forward for the first path cell >= DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST(~2400u) ahead, so while still finishing the WEST leg it aimed ~2400u up the NORTH leg -> he cut the inside of the corner diagonally and pinned EAST of the col-104 corridor against the canyon's east wall (the '^' cells in [ROUTEMAP] cols 105-109). Then the 2400u threshold sat right on the corner cell, so jitter flipped the steer target between the corner cell (-23040,-41472, aim SE, keys -D-R) and an up-leg cell (-23040,-37376, aim NW, keys U-L-) -- the #68 whipsaw -- and the reverse un-wedge just fed it (idx stuck 5/61, dist ~34350, until a random encounter interrupted at stuck-check 3/6). FIX (drive.inl, in the forward-lookahead scan, foot+vehicle): CORNER-CAP -- stop the lookahead at the current leg's APEX, i.e. the first cell j>idx where the path direction (4-connected sign of row/col step) bends away from the leg's initial direction AND the new direction PERSISTS (sustained >=2 cells, so a real L-corner, not a staircase). He then aims AT the corner, walks the leg into it, and turns up the next leg from INSIDE the corridor -- no diagonal cut, no pin. Straight/open legs still scan to the full ~2400u (stable bearing, no orbit -- preserves the .78 anti-orbit far target); staircases (alternating 1-cell steps) are not sustained so they keep the far target too. The monotonic path cursor (s_drivePathIdx only advances) + the .82 LOS clamp are unchanged. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + ROUTE_MAP_DIAG stay ON. BAT .100: from a Dollet start, drive to Timber -- EXPECT him to follow the WEST leg to the corner (X decreasing to ~-23040) WITHOUT cutting NW early, turn up the NORTH canyon, [YAWDRIVE] idx to CLIMB PAST 5 (the old wedge), the steer target to NOT flip between (-23040,-41472) and (-23040,-37376), and ideally continue toward Timber. REGRESSION: into-Dollet still arrives + a Balamb drive still routes/arrives (the cap only shortens the lookahead AT sustained corners; open routes keep the far target). If he reaches the corner but STILL pins (a real in-game wall at the apex, not just a cut), the next step is a LATERAL escape in the wedge recovery (the executor currently only presses toward-target or reverses; it never tries sideways to get around a wall) -- consider an F11 at the wedge to SEE open-ground-vs-wall. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.99: #70 FIX 3 -- PIVOT from the executor road-bridge to a PLANNER fix (the bridge was the wrong layer). The .98 BAT proved the bridge FIRES now but self-destructs: the nearest road cell world(-14848,-38400) is ~1424-1488u from the Dollet shelf start -- INSIDE WM_BRIDGE_ARRIVE_DIST(1536) -- so UpdateAutoDrive's arrive-check fires on the SAME tick the bridge is set (before the character moves), clears it, re-plans, re-pockets, re-bridges; that loop burns all 6 attempts in <1s at drive start, then direct-steers at Timber into the south wall (U--- JAM, 6 reverse-bursts N back into the pocket, gives up at dist~32716). The nearest road cell is also NE (toward Dollet) while Timber is SW -- the wrong direction. ROOT (answering 'why are we running into walls when the road should carry the route'): the #69 height-step guard EXEMPTS a step only when BOTH cells are road; the Dollet EXIT start sits on the coastal shelf fine(112,59), which is NOT a road cell, so the single step shelf->road is a non-road->road step that the guard blocks (the shelf->road elevation jump is big). So the BFS is sealed into a 5-cell pocket and NEVER reaches the canyon road -- [PLAN] 'start already the closest reachable cell (expanded 5) -- direct steer'. Driving IN works because the route is already road->road the whole way to the shelf edge + the #68 sweep hops the last cell; driving OUT needs the FIRST hop shelf->ROAD, which the guard forbade. FIX (planner.inl, 1 line): extend the guard exemption from 'both cells road' to 'DESTINATION cell is road' -- i.e. stepping ONTO a road from any adjacent cell is always allowed (the road is ground-truth walkable). Now the shelf connects to the adjacent road, the BFS gets on the canyon road, and road->road steps carry the route SW to Timber -- exactly the canyon route #69 was built for; the executor then follows a NORMAL path (no wall-running, no bridge). Stepping OFF a road onto a non-road cliff stays guarded (asymmetric -> no new cliff-climbing). Balamb is a road-less continent (no road cells) so the condition is identical to before there -> no regression. The .97/.98 executor bridge stays in the code but goes DORMANT: with the road connected the BFS now reaches Timber (dist~0, expanded large), so the pocket test (expanded<=40 AND dist>=6) cannot fire. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + ROUTE_MAP_DIAG stay ON for this BAT. BAT .99: from a Dollet start, drive to Timber -- EXPECT the [PLAN] line to change from 'start already the closest reachable cell ... expanded 5 -- direct steer' to '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells ... nearest reachable to target ... dist=0 (or small), expanded LARGE'; [ROUTEMAP] route to run SW along the canyon road (a chain of 'o' on road cells, NOT an 'S' pocket); [YAWDRIVE] idx to CLIMB past 0/0 as he follows the path; NO [DRIVE] Pocketed line; and ARRIVAL at Timber. REGRESSION: an into-Dollet drive still arrives AND a Balamb drive still routes/arrives (road-less, condition unchanged). If the [PLAN] still says 'expanded 5' the shelf's neighbours toward the road are themselves blocked (then connect via a slightly wider road-onramp rule); if it routes onto the road but the EXECUTOR wedges trying to physically walk shelf->road in-game (a real one-way cliff), THEN the executor needs the lateral sweep -- but the route will at least point the right way. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.98: #70 FIX 2 -- the .97 bridge-out NEVER FIRED. The .97 BAT still wedged 32km out; the world log showed why: the Dollet->Timber drive takes PlanPathFine's EARLY-RETURN-FALSE branch ([PLAN] Fine BFS: start already the closest reachable cell ... expanded 5 -- direct steer => return false), NOT the success branch the .97 bridge was built around. The PlanDrivePath bridge decision was gated `if (ok && ...)` (PlanPathFine TRUE), so with ok=false it was skipped: the planner fell to direct-steer at Timber, nosed into the sealing cliff, 6 reverse-bursts, gave up (dist~32716). The bridge has TWO pocket shapes (.96 success-branch dist=37 expanded=8 AND .97 early-return expanded=5) and only the first was handled. FIX (planner.inl, 2 surgical edits): (1) set the pocket signal (s_drivePlanExpanded/s_drivePlanDist) on the early-return-FALSE branch too -- it previously set them ONLY on the success path, so the bridge read stale values when ok=false; (2) drop the `ok &&` from the bridge gate so it keys PURELY on the pocket signal (expanded<=WM_POCKET_MAX_EXPANDED(40) AND dist>=WM_POCKET_MIN_DIST(6) AND under WM_BRIDGE_MAX(6) attempts) regardless of PlanPathFine's return. Safe: the bridge block is reached ONLY right after PlanPathFine runs (all earlier PlanDrivePath returns precede it) so the signals are always fresh; normal drives expand hundreds + reach dist~0 so the pocket test cannot false-trigger. NOTHING else changed: the executor (drive.inl StartAutoDrive bridge reset, UpdateAutoDrive bridge-arrive re-plan, steer-target override) + state.inl (consts s_drive*/WM_POCKET_*/WM_BRIDGE_*) + FindNearestRoadWorld were already complete and correct from .97 (the earlier 'planner stripped' belief was a stale read). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG re-enabled to watch the [YAWDRIVE] bridge steer toward the road cell (set false before push). LESSON: world_map_planner.inl is CRLF -- edit_file multi-line anchors MUST use \r\n. BAT .98: from a Dollet start, drive to Timber -- EXPECT [DRIVE] Pocketed start fine(112,59) (expanded 5, dist N) -> BRIDGE-OUT to nearest road cell at world(X,Y); [YAWDRIVE] steering toward that road cell (steer(X,Y) = the road cell); [DRIVE] Bridged out of pocket (reached road) -> re-planning to Timber; a NEW [PLAN] Fine path FROM the road cell that REACHES Timber (dist~0, expanded large); then ARRIVAL. REGRESSION: an into-Dollet drive must still arrive AND a Balamb drive must NOT print any [DRIVE] Pocketed line. If he reaches the road but the geometric-nearest road cell is across a cliff (wrong ramp), refine FindNearestRoadWorld next. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.97: #70 FIX (Option A) -- universal departure BRIDGE-OUT. The .96 diag confirmed (3 ways: [ROUTEMAP]+[ELEVMAP]+F11) Dollet sits in an ~8-cell pocket = its coastal shelf at elev -1287, SEALED IN by the #69 .90 height-step guard (6 blocked cliff edges around the start) + the .81 box to the south. Driving IN works because the route rides the ROAD (road-to-road edges are guard-EXEMPT) to the shelf edge, then the #68 sweep hops the last cell IN -- a ONE-WAY bridge. Driving OUT, the start is ON the shelf (not a road cell) so every edge off it is guard-blocked -> trapped (planner expands 8, dead-ends 37 short). FIX = mirror the sweep at DEPARTURE: PlanDrivePath now exposes the pocket signal (s_drivePlanExpanded + s_drivePlanDist set at the end of PlanPathFine) and, when expanded<=WM_POCKET_MAX_EXPANDED(40) AND dist>=WM_POCKET_MIN_DIST(6) AND under WM_BRIDGE_MAX(6) attempts, finds the nearest ROAD cell (FindNearestRoadWorld ring-search over s_roadFine, radius 30) and sets s_driveBridgeActive + s_driveBridgeX/Y. UpdateAutoDrive then STEERS at the road cell (steerX/Y override, not the dead-end route) with the normal yaw 8-way + reverse-burst; once within WM_BRIDGE_ARRIVE_DIST(1536u ~=1.5 cells) of it, drops bridge mode + re-plans from there (road edges guard-exempt -> fresh plan routes normally to target). Bridge state resets per drive in StartAutoDrive. Only fires for genuine pockets (normal drives expand hundreds + reach dist~0) so NO regression to into-Dollet or Balamb. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STAYS ON for this BAT. Files: state.inl (consts+state), planner.inl (signal+FindNearestRoadWorld+PlanDrivePath decision), drive.inl (StartAutoDrive reset, bridge-arrive re-plan, steer override). BAT .97: reach Dollet, then drive to Timber -- expect [DRIVE] Pocketed start ... BRIDGE-OUT to nearest road cell, Squall to walk toward the road (SW/lower-left green), [DRIVE] Bridged out of pocket -> re-planning, a NEW [PLAN] from the road cell that REACHES Timber (dist~0, expanded large) + [ROUTEMAP] route now heading SOUTH, then ARRIVAL. PLUS regression: into-Dollet still arrives + a Balamb drive (bridge must NOT fire on either -- no [DRIVE] Pocketed line). Bounded to 6 attempts so it can't loop. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.96: #70 DIAGNOSTIC build (no behavior change) -- SEE the Dollet EXIT pocket. #69 chain is PUSHED (main=v0.18.3.95, commit c591803b). NEW #70: auto-drive OUT of Dollet wedges -- from the Dollet start fine(111,57) the planner expands only 8 cells (a pocket) and dead-ends 37 short of Timber. Driving INTO Dollet works because the #68 final-approach sweep bridges the last bit IN; that bridge is ONE-WAY, so departure strands the planner inside the pocket. DECIDED this is NOT Dollet-exclusive (the sweep is a general fallback; manual-walk-into-any-pocket has the same failure; small-disconnected-component is a general 1024u-grid property) -> the fix is a UNIVERSAL departure-side bridge-out, NOT widening the .85 override. Before building it, this DIAGNOSTIC flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG 0->1 (LOCAL only; main stays clean at .95) so the [ROUTEMAP]/[ELEVMAP] at plan time reveal the pocket's exact walls + whether an open exit faces the target (the bridge must push a direction that LEAVES the pocket, else it re-wedges). BAT .96: reach Dollet (auto-drive), THEN auto-drive to Timber; send Logs/ff8_world.log. Read the [ROUTEMAP] from the Dollet->Timber plan: the 'S' pocket, the '^' walls around it (incl the .81 box rows 59-69), and whether a '.' corridor opens south toward the target. That shapes the bridge-out (next build): when expanded<<dist, steer toward the target/nearest big-component cell with the executor+reverse-burst until the current cell joins the main component, then re-plan. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.95: #69 -- diagnostics OFF for the push. Flipped ROUTE_MAP_DIAG 1->0 in world_map_planner.inl, silencing the per-plan [ROUTEMAP]/[ELEVSTEP]/[ELEVMAP] dumps (DumpRouteMap + the elev logs compile out; elevBlockedEdges stays [[maybe_unused]]). NO behavior change -- routing/executor identical to .94 (which reached Dollet). This is the pre-push build: the #69 chain (.88-onward) is now ready to land on main (v0.18.3.87) as ONE commit, with the .90 height-step guard + .88 steepness as the real wins, the .81 AABB + .85 override KEPT (both load-bearing), and only WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY retired. BAT .95: confirm it still BUILDS CLEAN (watch for unused-variable warnings now the diag blocks compile out) and the Galbadia->Dollet on-foot drive still ARRIVES with the [ROUTEMAP]/[ELEVSTEP] lines GONE from ff8_world.log; PLUS a Balamb regression. If clean, push via Utilities/push_to_github.ps1 (Aaron only). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.94: #69 build 7 -- RESTORE the .85 road-walkable override (the actual cause of the .92/.93 regression). Reading the INITIAL plan (not a re-plan) settled it: from the SAME save start fine(99,72) that reached Dollet in .91, the .93 plan reported Dollet UNREACHABLE -- "Fine path: 14 cells ... nearest reachable to target fine(112,57), dist=15", a dead-end 15 cells short, wedging against the .81 box wall. The ONLY routing change from working-.91 to broken-.93 was removing the .85 override (offroad was already 0 -- a no-op). The Timber->Dollet road runs THROUGH the .81 box (cols 104-111 rows 59-69); the .85 override carves the road cells back to walkable so the route threads the box to Dollet, and without it the .81 patch walls the road off and DISCONNECTS Dollet. So BOTH the .81 AABB and the .85 override are LOAD-BEARING; only WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY (=0, no-op) was safely retired. This build restores the .85 override (segments.inl, right after the .81 block) -- back to the proven .91 routing. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STILL ON. BAT .94: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet from the usual save -- the [PLAN] Fine path must now REACH Dollet (dist~0, ~31 cells like .91) and ARRIVE; the [ROADMAP] override line should report road cells forced walkable; PLUS Balamb (B-Garden + Fire Cavern). If it arrives, the NEXT build flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false + pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward; .81 + .85 kept, only offroad retired) as ONE commit. LESSON: the #69 cleanup over-reached -- mechanism 2 (.90 height-step guard) improves general routing but does NOT replace the Dollet hardcodes; both stay. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.93: #69 build 6 -- RESTORE the .81 Dollet false-coast AABB (build 5 regression fix). The .92 BAT (which retired .81 + .85 + offroad) REGRESSED: with the .81 patch gone, the [ROUTEMAP] route ran straight through the ex-ledge (cols 104-110 = the AABB's old box) instead of the west lane, and the on-foot drive wedged ~15km out near the start (moved 62u, stuck ~fine(106,70)). [ELEVSTEP] still blocked 614 cliff edges and [ELEVMAP] still showed the 1513-unit cliff FACE -- mechanism 2 IS working -- but the ledge is impassable in-game where its elevation is CONTINUOUS (the route enters it via a <400 step), so the height-step guard cannot exclude it. That is exactly the original #67 finding (no pure-geometry rule separates this ledge from real land): mechanism 2 catches the cliff face, not the ledge approach. So the .81 AABB is LOAD-BEARING and is RESTORED (the forced-steep-mountain box in segments.inl + the DOLLET_COAST_* consts in state.inl). KEPT RETIRED from .92 (both confirmed safe): the .85 road-walkable override (the .92 [ROUTEMAP] showed the west lane walkable without it) and WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY (was already 0 -- a no-op). Net vs the working .91: only the .85 override + the offroad const/term are gone; routing is otherwise the proven .91 path. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STILL ON. BAT .93: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet -- MUST ARRIVE again, [ROUTEMAP] route back on the west lane avoiding the '^' ex-ledge box (now re-blocked); PLUS Balamb (B-Garden + Fire Cavern) regression. If it arrives clean, the NEXT build flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false + pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward; .81 kept, .85 + offroad retired) as ONE commit. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.92: #69 build 5 -- CLEANUP: retire the road-crutch scaffolding now that mechanism 2 carries the route. The .91 BAT proved it (Galbadia->Dollet route SAME shape with the road cost-preference gone, stayed off the false-coast '^' band; Balamb B-Garden + Fire Cavern regression still arrived). This build REMOVES: (1) the .85 road-walkable override (segments.inl -- it forced s_roadFine cells to SEG_LAND steep=0); (2) the .81 DOLLET_COAST AABB (the forced-steep-mountain patch in segments.inl + the DOLLET_COAST_* consts in state.inl); (3) WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY (planner.inl -- the const + its + term; already 0 since .91 so removing it is a no-op). KEPT: the height-step guard's road-to-road exemption (harmless -- it only skips the guard between two genuinely-walkable road cells; removing it is an unnecessary risk, so it stays and keeps reading s_roadFine). EFFECT TO TEST: the .81 AABB removal un-blocks the false-coast ledge cells as walkable NODES again, so the route now relies ENTIRELY on the height-step EDGE guard to stay off them -- in [ROUTEMAP] the ledge cells should flip from '^' back to '.' but still carry NO 'o' (route avoids them because the ~1570-unit cliff EDGES are guard-blocked, >400). ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STAYS ON for this BAT so we can SEE that. BAT .92: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet -- MUST still ARRIVE; [ROUTEMAP] route must still avoid the ledge (now '.' not '^') with no 'o' on it; [ELEVSTEP] blocked>0 (the guard now does ALL the cliff exclusion). PLUS Balamb on-foot (B-Garden + Fire Cavern) regression. Two failure modes the [ROUTEMAP] distinguishes: route cuts onto the ex-ledge -> the guard missed an entry edge (re-add the .81 AABB); route fails at a road pinch -> the .85 override removal blocked a needed cell (re-add it). If clean, the NEXT build flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false and pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward) as ONE commit. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.91: #69 build 4 -- RETIRE the road's cost-preference so the height-step guard (mechanism 2) does the routing. .90 proved the guard: it blocked 629 cliff edges and [ELEVMAP] showed the real false-coast cliff = a 1570-unit adjacent step vs WM_CLIMB_STEP=400 (walkable steps <400, Dollet reached) -- 400 well-centered. But the route still leaned on the road (the .86 cost preference: road step = cost 1, clearance-exempt). This build DROPS that branch in PlanPathFine: EVERY cell now pays the same clearance+steepness cost, and WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY 20->0 -- so the route is shaped by GEOMETRY (guard excludes cliffs, clearance centres the corridor, steepness pulls to the flat floor), not by the road being cheaper. KEPT this build (dropped as cleanup in build 5): the height-step guard's road-to-road exemption, the .85 road-walkable override, and the .81 DOLLET_COAST AABB -- so if Dollet stops arriving it was the cost change, not those. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG on. BAT .91: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet -- MUST still ARRIVE, now routed by the guard+clearance not the road's cost advantage; the [ROUTEMAP] route must still avoid every '^'/false-land cell (the guard, not the road, keeps it off); [ELEVSTEP] blocked>0. PLUS a Balamb on-foot regression (road-less continent; should be ~identical since the offroad penalty was its only road-related term). If Dollet still reached + clean route, mechanism 2 is carrying the route alone -> build 5 drops the .85 override + .81 AABB + the now-dead road-to-road exemption + the offroad const, sets ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false, and pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward) as ONE commit. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.90: #69 build 3 -- MECHANISM 2: height-step edge adjacency (the CORE terrain-ID fix). New per-cell s_elevFine (avg of the poly's 3 vertex elevations) populated in RasterizeTriFine alongside s_steepFine; PlanPathFine now blocks an inter-cell edge when the absolute floor-height difference |s_elevFine[a]-s_elevFine[b]| > WM_CLIMB_STEP (=400, tunable) so a false-coast cliff SELF-EXCLUDES even though both cells read 'land' -- the grid is NODE-based, the cliff is an EDGE. Road-to-road steps are EXEMPT (keeps the Timber->Dollet ribbon connected while WM_CLIMB_STEP is calibrated; stepping OFF the road onto a non-road cliff is still guarded). Diagnostics (gated ROUTE_MAP_DIAG): [ELEVSTEP] logs how many cliff edges the guard blocked during expansion; [ELEVMAP] (in DumpRouteMap) logs the window's max adjacent elev-step + location + the S/D cell elevations, so the REAL false-coast cliff-step magnitude is visible to set WM_CLIMB_STEP. This is the geometry-based replacement that will let later builds retire the .85/.86 road crutch + the .81 Dollet AABB. BAT .90: on-foot far (>=15km) Galbadia->Dollet -- the route should STILL reach Dollet (the road carries it via the exemption, so no disconnect risk yet); expect [ELEVSTEP] blocked>0 (guard firing) and [ELEVMAP] to reveal the cliff step at the false coast; ALSO Balamb on-foot regression (route ~identical; guard only blocks genuine cliff edges). If blocked>0 + Dollet reached + no [ROUTEMAP] cut onto a '^'/false-land cell, mechanism 2 is wired correctly and build 4 retires the road crutch (offroad->0, drop .85/.86) so the guard does the real work; if the [ELEVMAP] cliff step is far above/below 400, tune WM_CLIMB_STEP. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.89: #69 build 2 -- WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY 40->20 (start retiring the road's flat cost-advantage; road stays clearance-exempt so it still wins the corridor; ROUTE_MAP_DIAG ON). BAT .89: far Galbadia->Dollet (route ~unchanged, no cut off the road onto a false-land/'^' cell) + Balamb regression. // v0.18.3.88: #69 build 1 -- steepness-weighted planning (steepPen = s_steepFine/8 on the non-road step cost only; road still cost 1, additive, no Dollet regression). BAT'd CLEAN: far ~13.2km (dist=13235) Galbadia->Dollet on-foot, [ROUTEMAP] threaded the canyon up the open lane to Dollet hugging steep~=0, arrived sweep-assisted (= the #68 executor end-game, expected). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.87: #67 -- YAW-BASED SCREEN-RELATIVE 8-WAY on-foot steering (the executor fix the road work isolated). The .86 road-preference BAT SOLVED ROUTING: the [PLAN]/[ROUTEMAP] route follows road cells up col 99 then bends east along the road to Dollet, and F11 showed Squall PHYSICALLY STANDING ON THE PAVED ROAD -- but he still wedged at the START (idx=0/32, ~19.5km from this farther save), oscillating, and the trace shows EXACTLY why and it is NOT routing: he is ON the road, the road runs due north here (tgtBrg ~4070 ~= 358deg), but hdg=3752 is FROZEN (the fixed per-region CAMERA YAW ~= 330deg), so the .80 heading turn-then-go pressed pure UP -- which on foot WALKS at the yaw (NNW), ~28deg off the road -- and that drift carried him off the thin road ribbon into the cliff beside it (d+0 on contact; reverse shoved him back; UP walked him into it again). The heading-law can only press pure cardinals (UP, or R/L pivot), so it can never aim the DIAGONAL needed to track a road bearing between N and NE. This is the .80 unify failure: .80 routed on-foot onto the vehicle heading law on the (correct) finding that UP walks at the yaw, but a yaw you can READ is not a facing you can STEER -- L/R are screen-relative WALK keys, not a rotation, so turn-then-go is meaningless on foot. FIX (the correct use of .79's camera-yaw disassembly, which .80 misused): steer in SCREEN space. screenAngle = (targetBearing - yaw) & 0xFFF is the target's direction relative to screen-up (= what UP walks); press the NEAREST OF 8 ARROW COMBOS (cardinals + diagonals: sector = ((screenAngle+256)>>9)&7, UP / UP+RIGHT / RIGHT / DOWN+RIGHT / DOWN / DOWN+LEFT / LEFT / UP+LEFT) so he walks toward the target and STAIRCASES along the road's bends. At the .86 wedge screenAngle ~= +318 (28deg CW of screen-up) -> sector 1 = UP+RIGHT -> walks NNE, AWAY from the cliff and along the road, instead of NNW into it. The on-foot branch is RE-SPLIT off the vehicle heading law (the dead if(false&&isOnFoot) screen-basis/probe block stays as dead code; a new else-if(isOnFoot) holds the yaw steering; vehicles keep the unchanged heading turn-then-go else). Routing/cursor/steer-target+LOS-clamp/stuck/replan/reverse-burst/final-approach ALL unchanged; the reverse-burst un-wedge (DOWN=screen-down) is kept for foot recovery. New [YAWDRIVE] trace logs yaw/tgtBrg/screenAngle/keys. ONE handedness assumption: RIGHT walks screen-right = yaw+90 CW; if the BAT shows him tracking bends the WRONG way (steering consistently to the wrong side at a turn), swap RIGHT<->LEFT (one line). BAT .87: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [YAWDRIVE] to press UP+RIGHT (not pure UP) at the start, Squall to walk NNE ALONG the road instead of NNW into the cliff, idx to CLIMB past 0, dist to fall past 19.5km, ideally ARRIVAL staircasing along the road; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- vehicles are untouched, but confirm foot still works on a road-less continent where the route is open ground). Watch: (a) handedness (wrong-way at a turn -> swap R/L); (b) do 2-key diagonals combine into diagonal movement (if not, he'll jerk between the two cardinals -- still nets toward target, but the fallback is time-multiplexed cardinal staircasing). If he now follows the road but stalls at a TIGHT bend, that's a local-geometry/lookahead tune, not the steering model. After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.86: #67 -- ROAD-PREFERENCE in the planner (build 3 of the road pivot; makes the route FOLLOW the road instead of leaving it). The .85 road-walkable override BAT SOLVED routing in principle (the planner now CAN route the road -- 623 road cells forced walkable, the ribbon reconnected) but the drive still wedged ~13.5km out at the START, oscillating, idx=0/20, never advancing. F11 at the wedge = Squall at the FOOT OF A COASTAL CLIFF, and the [ROADMAP]/[ROUTEMAP] showed why: the start fine(101,65) IS a road cell (row 65 cols 100-105 are road) and the road winds continuously NE up to Dollet, BUT the route LEFT the road at fine(101,63) -- which is LAND, not road -- where the road jogs EAST to cols 104-107; the planner cut straight up col 101 onto the non-road false-land cliff (a couple cells shorter than following the road's bend) and drove into the rock. ROOT: the clearance-weighted Dijkstra had NO road awareness -- it cut corners off the road wherever a non-road shortcut was shorter, straight into the false-land cliffs the road exists to avoid. FIX: road-preference in PlanPathFine's step cost -- a step onto a road cell (s_roadFine, already populated unconditionally since .84) costs 1 with NO clearance penalty (the road IS the corridor, needs no centering); a step onto a NON-road cell pays the clearance penalty PLUS a flat WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY=40, so any multi-cell off-road shortcut loses to staying on the road and the route FOLLOWS the road's bends to Dollet. Penalty is finite, so non-road terrain stays usable for the short start/end hops and on road-less continents (Balamb: with no road cells competing, every step pays the same flat penalty -> relative costs and the route are UNCHANGED -- no regression). This addresses the .81 lesson that road-attraction fights the clearance penalty by ZEROING the clearance term on road cells. NOTE the on-foot EXECUTOR is still .80's heading turn-then-go, which can't truly steer on foot (hdg=0x0203ED02 is the fixed per-region CAMERA YAW, not a controllable facing; the .85 trace showed it frozen at 3783 pressing U into the cliff) -- but at the .85 wedge BOTH the heading-law and a correct yaw-based steerer would press the same arrow (target ~16deg off screen-up), so the EXECUTOR was not the differentiator there; the ROUTE pointing into the cliff was. Once the route follows the road (real walkable ground), if the executor then mis-steers at the road's turns, the NEXT build is the yaw-based screen-relative steering (read the yaw at 0x0203ED02, arrow = nearest(targetBearing - yaw); replaces .80's turn-then-go on foot, vehicles keep the heading law) -- the correct use of the camera-yaw disassembly, which .80 misused as a steerable facing. BAT .86: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect the [ROUTEMAP] route to STAY on the road (follow the eastward jog at fine(101,63)->cols 104-107, NOT cut straight up col 101 into the cliff), the drive to actually progress past 13.5km along the road, and ideally ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- road-preference changes every planned route's cost, though road-less routes should be identical). If the route now hugs the road but the executor still oscillates at a road turn, that isolates the executor -> do the yaw-based steering next. After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.85: #67 -- ROAD-WALKABLE OVERRIDE (build 2 of the road pivot; the executor/planner now route on the reconnected road). The .84 [ROADMAP] dump CONFIRMED the road: terrain types 27/28 present (3605+1875 polys), 623 road fine cells, the ribbon reaches Dollet (R adjacent to D at fine(113,57)), and the Dollet-drive start fine(102,64) sits ON the road. It also revealed (a) WHY the planner kept hitting the cliff -- the road does NOT go straight up fine col 102; at rows 62-64 it jogs EAST to cols 104-107 and climbs to Dollet from there, while col 102 reads as walkable LAND in the grid but is the in-game cliff (and col 102 dead-ends into ocean at row 58, so straight-north was never a through path); and (b) that a large stretch of the road was being FORCE-BLOCKED by our own grid -- the [ROADMAP] '!' (road-over-blocked) cells cluster at rows 59-69 cols 104-111, EXACTLY the AABB the .81 Dollet false-coast patch blocks (the patch sits on top of the real road), plus a few road cells running through steep-mtn-classified cells. THIS build makes the road authoritative: after rasterization AND the .81 patch, every s_roadFine cell is forced to SEG_LAND steep=0 (walkable), overriding both the steep-mtn misclassification and the .81 patch wherever they fall on the road -- so the road becomes one continuous walkable ribbon from the start to Dollet, while non-road cells (incl. the rest of the false ledge) keep their classification. Applied BEFORE the clearance BFS so reachability/clearance/planner all see the road as the open corridor; through the cliff pinch the road is now the ONLY walkable path, so the existing clearance-weighted Dijkstra is funneled onto it with NO planner rewrite. New [ROADMAP] 'road-walkable override' log line reports how many road cells were forced (and how many had been blocked). The [ROADMAP] dump (still on) now renders the previously-'!' cells as 'R' = built-in confirmation. BAT .85: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect the [ROADMAP] override line (wasBlk>0, ~the .81 patch's road cells + steep-mtn road cells), the dump's '!' cells flipped to 'R', the [PLAN]/[ROUTEMAP] route to jog EAST onto the road around the col-102 cliff (not drive straight into it), dist past 12km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- road-forcing changes the grid, though Balamb's route is on a different continent). If the route now follows the road but the EXECUTOR wanders off it in the OPEN stretches (steering works on open ground per .66, so this is unlikely to block), build 3 adds an explicit road-preference/restriction to the planner. If he still wedges, the [HDG-DIAG]/[ROUTEMAP] show whether it's the route (still off the road) or the executor (on the road but mis-steering). After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.84: #67 -- ROAD extraction (load-only diagnostic; PIVOT from inferred-terrain steering to the road as ground-truth). Three steering BATs (.80/.82/.83) all wedged ~12km because the planner routes up col 102 into an in-game CLIFF that the 1024u grid mis-classifies as walkable LAND -- confirmed by F11 (Squall at the foot of a sheer rock face) + the trace (he walks freely north to the row-63/64 boundary then cannot cross; reverse frees him south; he never enters fine row 63). ROOT (answer to 'why do we keep hitting mountains'): the mod does NOT read true walkability -- it rasterizes wmx polygons into a coarse 1024u grid and GUESSES walkable from terrain-type + a steepness threshold (WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK) tuned for reachability not collision; both guesses fail at cliffs (a cliff face + the flat ground at its base land in adjacent cells; the rasterizer marks a cell land if any land poly touches it) and narrow passes. Aaron's fix (correct): the wmx tags ROAD polys (terrain 27/28 = Road/Railroad) -- the one signal both reliably walkable AND running exactly Timber->Dollet. Route ALONG the road instead of inferring terrain, and use the road as ground-truth to refine the grid (road cells = walkable; off-road 'land' cells wedged between the road and a mountain band are suspect cliff). THIS build is step 1, pure observation, ZERO behavior change: LoadTerrainGrid now captures road polys into a new s_roadFine[192][256] overlay (RasterizeTriRoad: centre-in-triangle PLUS the 3 vertex + centroid cells, so a sub-cell-thin ribbon still registers), tallies a terrain-type histogram, and (ROAD_MAP_DIAG) logs [ROADMAP]: road fine-cell count, the type:polys histogram (confirms 27/28 exist + how many), and an ASCII map of the Dollet corridor (rows 45..76 cols 88..121) with the road ribbon over the fine class grid (R=road, !=road-over-a-cell-we-currently-block, D=Dollet, ~/./f/m/^). BAT .84: load a GALBADIA world-map save (NO driving needed -- the dump fires once at terrain load), send Logs/ff8_world.log. Read [ROADMAP]: (1) histogram confirms road=27/28 present; (2) road fine cells > 0 and the ribbon forms a continuous chain to D; (3) any '!' = road cells we wrongly block. Build 2 then routes the Dollet drive ALONG s_roadFine + forces road cells walkable + flags suspect off-road cliff cells. ROAD_MAP_DIAG retires before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.83: #67 -- FORWARD-COLLISION GUARD on the steering law (a step-back redesign toward terrain-reactive execution). The .82 LOS-clamp WORKED (the steer target moved to the clamped near cell fine(102,63), confirmed in [HDG-DIAG] steer(-26112,-33280)) but he STILL wedged ~12km out at the SAME cliff corner, and the trace pinpointed the proximate cause: target NNW, hdg due-north 36, off=318 -- JUST inside the ~320 STEER_DEADZONE -- so the law chose STRAIGHT and walked him due north into the blocked fine(103,63) the route goes WEST around; the stuck-reverse backed him off then the unchanged law walked him right back (reverse->re-aim->re-wedge), hdg frozen at 36 the entire time (he never pivoted because off never exceeded the deadzone). Re-eval (Aaron-requested): the executor is an open-loop bearing-follower BLIND to the terrain directly in front of the character -- no target-selection tweak fixes that, because 'roughly aimed' within the deadzone can still be aimed into a wall. FIX (first step toward reactive local avoidance): before the final STRAIGHT branch commits UP, probe the fine cell ~1 cell ahead of the CURRENT facing (heading->world vector, one WM_FINE_CELL out); if it's blocked for foot/car (SEG_OCEAN or steep SEG_MOUNTAIN), set fwdGuard and press toward the target side (err sign: RIGHT if err>=0 else LEFT) instead of UP. On this region's screen-relative controls (hdg is the FIXED per-region camera yaw -- frozen at 36 through STRAIGHT/PIVOT/REVERSE incl. a 600u reverse, the .73 behavior, NOT .80's rotate-the-facing) that walks him sideways WEST into the open col-102 corridor; on a turn-then-go region it pivots him there. The guard reads the grid in front of him so it fires ONLY near a wall -- open-road steering + the deadzone are unchanged (no orbit/oscillation regression, preserves .60/.78). Reuses the existing pivot key choice; no new state, no planner/target change (the .82 clamp stays). [HDG-DIAG] gains a GUARD marker (a PIVOT line with off<=deadzone + GUARD = the guard fired). BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] at the corner to show GUARD with keys=--L- (or --R-) when the cell ahead is blocked, him to move WEST around the cliff instead of pinning on it, dist past 12km, ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- the guard is in every planned drive's steering, but only acts adjacent to a wall). If he moves west but then can't make the next turn, the next step is the full feeler-fan controller (probe several headings, pick the open one best toward target) + a blocked-direction memory for convergent recovery. If he STILL pins, the trace shows whether GUARD fired and which key -- tells us if the issue is the guard or the control model. After a clean BAT set all #67 diags false and push .54-onward as ONE commit. // v0.18.3.82: #67 -- TIGHT-CORNER navigation: clamp the steer target to a clear line of sight + give on-foot the REVERSE un-wedge. The .81 patch WORKED (route went up the west canyon, dist 15.8km->11km) but he wedged at a real cliff corner fine(103,64): the F11 shows him pressed against a rock massif with open grass to his WEST, and [HDG-DIAG] shows the steer target aimed NNW (-26112,-35328) straight ACROSS the blocked fine(103,63) cliff that the winding route goes AROUND, hdg frozen at 36 (due north), pos sawing east-west, idx stuck at 0/29. Root: (1) the far ~2400u lookahead target sat on the far side of a cliff corner, so he pressed north INTO the rock; on the world map's tank controls rotation needs forward motion, so nosed into terrain he could neither advance nor pivot out. (2) WORSE -- on-foot had NO working recovery: the .80 unify routed on-foot STEERING to the vehicle heading law (if (false && isOnFoot) dead-branched the screen-relative path) but left on-foot RECOVERY pointing at the SIDESTEP, whose only consumer is inside that dead branch -- so the stuck check logged 'SIDESTEP left' and pressed NOTHING. TWO complementary fixes (separable in the trace): (a) wire the pre-written FineLineClearFootCar clamp -- walk the lookahead target back to the farthest route cell reachable by a clear straight line, so he aims ALONG the open corridor (here due WEST around the cliff) and turns there in open ground BEFORE reaching the rock; engages only near walls (open ground keeps the far target -> no orbit, preserves .78). (b) on-foot stuck recovery now fires the SAME DOWN reverse-burst as vehicles (tank controls: only reverse frees a nosed-in character; a lateral sidestep can't, and DOWN moves him on foot per .71) -- backs him off into open ground where the pivot can rotate him around the corner. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] steer target to track the open corridor (NOT a fixed NNW point across a cliff), keys=---D reverse bursts if he does wedge, dist past 11km, ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes lookahead + recovery for every planned drive). If he still wedges: the trace shows whether the clamp moved the steer target (steerX/Y) and whether reverse fired (D key) -- tells us which half to push. After a clean BAT set all #67 diags false and push .54-onward as ONE commit. // v0.18.3.81: #67 -- HARDCODED Dollet false-coast no-walk patch (Aaron-approved one-off). After the .80 heading-unify BAT still wedged ~15.8km out at the east->north coastal corner, offline wmx.obj analysis found the ROOT cause is the PLANNER, not the executor: the route runs up a thin coastal cliff ledge SE of Dollet that reads as walkable LAND in wmx but is impassable on foot in-game, and NO geometric rule separates it from real land (validated offline across 7 rules -- blanket cliff-block AND coastal-ledge-block both DISCONNECT same-continent Dollet from the start; 512-res reconnects the road but breaks the Balamb Town->Fire Cavern regression; road-attraction reroutes only with the clearance penalty disabled and is parameter-fragile). The Timber->Dollet canyon road (wmx terrain types 27/28) one cell WEST is the real foot approach; the planner ignored it because the fine grid discards the road tag and the ledge looked more direct. Since Dollet is a one-off (coast on one side, canyon road on the other), LoadTerrainGrid now marks the ledge AABB impassable by COORDINATE (DOLLET_COAST_X0/X1/Y0/Y1 in world_map_state.inl), implemented as forced-steep MOUNTAIN (steep=0xFFFF) so the existing foot/car block rule (cls==SEG_MOUNTAIN && steep>WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK) catches it with ZERO change to IsFineTraversable; Garden/Ragnarok bypass the fine grid so are unaffected. Offline-validated against the LIVE clearance-weighted planner cost: with the patch the route runs WEST up the canyon to Dollet (not north up the coast) and Dollet stays reachable; Balamb is untouched (different cells, different continent). BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [TERRAIN] 'Dollet false-coast patch: blocked N fine cells', the [ROUTEMAP] route to run west/up the canyon rather than north up the coast, dist to drop past 15.8km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes the Galbadia-area grid only, but confirm Balamb destinations still route). If it arrives, set all #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) false and push the .54-onward chain as one commit. // v0.18.3.80: #67 -- UNIFY on-foot steering onto the heading-based turn-then-go (the vehicle law). The v0.18.3.79 [YAWPROBE] BAT was decisive and POSITIVE: with the character actually walking (footsteps confirmed), holding UP walked a STRAIGHT line whose COMPASS bearing == the camera yaw (0x0203ED02), matched within 1deg at TWO different yaws in the SAME run (the camera rotated mid-run: yaw 316deg -> walked 315deg; yaw 355deg -> walked 356deg); holding UP did NOT rotate the camera (yaw steady through each forward burst); and RIGHT INCREASED the yaw (clockwise). So 0x0203ED02 is the LIVE on-foot facing -- NOT frozen (the .72/.73 'frozen' reads were the character wedged/pressing UP into terrain, which doesn't rotate) and NOT screen-relative-per-region. The relationship has ZERO offset in the game's own bearing units: desiredYaw == the target's TorusBearing (0=N, CW), exactly what the existing VEHICLE steering already computes (err=TorusBearing-heading; if |off|>STEER_DEADZONE pivot RIGHT when err>=0 since RIGHT raises the yaw, else LEFT; else UP) and which already works for cars. FIX: the on-foot executor branch (the .74-.78 screen-basis + .77 greedy arrow-probe, built on the now-disproven frozen-heading reading) is BYPASSED -- gated `if (false && isOnFoot)`, left in place as dead code like the retired DCAL calibration -- so on foot now falls through to the SAME heading turn-then-go as vehicles. No other behavior change; routing/cursor/waypoint/stuck/replan/sweep/reverse-burst/final-approach all unchanged. The on-foot [HDG-DIAG] trace (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG) now logs hdg/tgtBrg/err/off/keys/band for the foot drive. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] hdg to track tgtBrg, err to shrink as he aligns then STRAIGHT frames carrying him along the route, dist dropping past 15.8km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this routes foot through the previously vehicle-only branch). If he wedges at a tight corner and can't pivot out (the old .66-.72 failure mode: turning needs a little open space), that is now a GEOMETRY/recovery issue, not a steering-law issue -- the clearance router already avoids tight corners and the reverse-burst un-wedge exists; tune those, do NOT revert to the probe. Set all #67 diags false before the push. // v0.18.3.79: #67 -- DIAGNOSTIC build, no steering change. Offline FF8_EN.exe disassembly (2026-06-22) located the world-map CAMERA: 0x0203ED02 = camera YAW (0..4096 = 360deg; live value 501 ~= 44deg), fed into the view-matrix builder at .text 0x552D20 (PSX RotMatrix/ApplyMatrix lib), SET on region transitions (writers 0x548155/0x54A1C8/0x54D030) and eased toward a target by a follow routine at 0x558590 (<=16 units/frame); 0x0203FE52 = camera PITCH (~45deg downward tilt). KEY REFRAME: the mod has been reading 0x0203ED02 ALL ALONG as GetWorldMapHeading() and wrongly dismissing it as a frozen, useless 'character heading' -- it is the CAMERA YAW, frozen on foot because the on-foot world-map camera holds a FIXED yaw PER REGION, which is exactly why holding one arrow walks a STRAIGHT line whose WORLD direction differs by region (Aaron-confirmed). This supersedes the entire heading/basis/probe steering arc (.66-.78): with the yaw readable, navigation needs no empirical basis and no orbit-prone probe -- read the yaw each frame, rotate the world->target bearing by -yaw, press the nearest arrow, re-read on region change. This build REPLACES the F12 diagnostic body only (world_map_camera_scan.inl, SAME external symbols TriggerCameraScan/UpdateCameraScan/CamScanPause/CamScanResume so dinput8.cpp + Poll() need no rewiring): retired the dead .73 G/H camera-rotate scan; new [YAWPROBE] holds UP ~1.2s (sampling pos+yaw every 250ms), then RIGHT, then UP again, logging the net world vector per hold + the computed offset(UP-walk bearing vs yaw) + turn sense. It CONFIRMS the yaw drives movement and MEASURES the one constant the steering rewrite needs. BAT: on the world map standing STILL (not driving), press F12, hold still ~6s (fully automated, TTS on start/complete; if a random encounter interrupts, fight it and the probe auto-restarts), send Logs/ff8_world.log; then WALK into the next region and press F12 again -- in the [YAWPROBE] SUMMARY the yaw should DIFFER between regions but offsetUP should MATCH (proves yaw-driven movement) and sense(RIGHT-UP) should be ~+/-90. No steering/routing change this build. Next build = the yaw-based steering rewrite using the measured offset. CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG + the remaining #67 diags retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.78: #67 -- fix the greedy-probe ORBIT by aiming at a FAR route target instead of the adjacent cell. The .77 BAT (greedy arrow-probe, built+run) proved the PROBE STEERING WORKS -- it correctly presses cardinals and moves him on open ground -- but he STILL hung ~15.8km out, and the trace + F11 screenshot revealed why, and it's NOT a wall: the F11 shows him standing on open green grass with a clear field to the north (cliffs/Dollet road far in the background), and the [SRDIAG] trace shows idx=0/22 the WHOLE time (cursor never advances past the first route cell) with tgtBrg swinging wildly (231->255->329->152->...) every quarter-second. That swing is the signature of ORBITING: the steer target was the IMMEDIATELY ADJACENT route cell (DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS=1, ~1024u away), so every ~180u walk step swung the bearing to it ~15deg, the probe re-decided every 250ms window, and he circled the first waypoint on row 70 instead of committing to a direction and crossing north into the open field. FIX: aim the steer target at the first route cell at least DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST (~2400u) ahead along the path (the OLD pre-.64 rule), not the adjacent cell. A ~2400u target swings only ~4deg per step, so the bearing is STABLE and the probe locks onto the northward cardinal and carries him up the corridor, advancing the cursor. The far target was cut to 1 cell back in .64 ONLY because the THEN heading-pivot steering drove straight at a far target and jammed into walls -- the greedy probe does NOT jam (it rejects walled cardinals via the <MIN_MOVE test and staircases around obstacles), so a far target is now both SAFE and NECESSARY to stop the orbit. One change; the probe logic, cursor advance, stuck/replan, sidestep, and vehicle path are all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] expect tgtBrg to now hold ROUGHLY STEADY (not swing 100s of units every line), idx to CLIMB past 0 as he advances up the route, dist to drop past 15.8km, and him to walk north across the open field toward the canyon instead of circling; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes every planned drive's target selection). If he still orbits, raise DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST further or lower DRIVE_PROBE_ALIGN_MIN so the probe will take wider detours; if he commits to a direction but jams against real geometry, that's a grid/route issue at that cell. Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.77: #67 -- PIVOT the on-foot steering executor off the maintained screen->world basis to a GREEDY EMPIRICAL ARROW PROBE. Three BATs (.74 stale basis / .75 corrupt basis / .76 guard-frozen basis) all produced the SAME outcome: clean eastward progress to ~15.8km then sawing east-west at the route corner, never turning north -- which per the project's own rule (3 identical outcomes = wrong diagnosis, pivot) means the maintained-basis approach is the wrong tool. The .76 trace was decisive: even with the guard HOLDING the basis stable at uHat~(1,0), pressing the basis-predicted U-L- combo drove him WEST (basis predicted NNE) and he oscillated dist 15887<->17404 on row 70, never going north. A predicted screen->world basis is unreliable exactly where the camera swings (the corner), and 2-key diagonals don't combine as the basis assumes. The route itself is FINE (planner threads the gap; DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS=1 aims at the adjacent next cell) -- the executor just can't convert 'go toward that cell' into the right arrow. NEW APPROACH: trust ONLY measured progress. Hold ONE cardinal arrow for DRIVE_PROBE_WINDOW_MS(250); measure the window's displacement; KEEP the arrow if it both moved him (>=DRIVE_PROBE_MIN_MOVE=40u) AND lined up with the target dir (align >= DRIVE_PROBE_ALIGN_MIN=0.55, ~within 57deg); else ROTATE to the next cardinal (UP->RIGHT->DOWN->LEFT). No basis, no trig, no diagonals -- immune to camera swing and wall-slide (it only believes what it measures). At the corner this naturally STAIRCASES: the walled direction fails the MIN_MOVE test and it takes the open cardinal that actually progresses toward the next route cell. DRIVE_PROBE_MAX_FAILS(8) non-progressing windows in a row = genuine pocket -> re-plan. Calibration (the UP/RIGHT probe) is DISARMED (its basis is now unused); RefreshBasisAxis/OrthonormalizeAgainst/the basis state are now dead (left in place, like prior unused helpers). Cursor/steer-target/sidestep/stuck/replan/vehicle path all UNCHANGED. [SRDIAG] now logs arrow=UP/RIGHT/DOWN/LEFT + fails count instead of uHat/rHat. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [SRDIAG] to settle on a single arrow that drops dist, STAIRCASE through the corner (arrow flips between two cardinals as it rounds it) WITHOUT the backward rout, and progress past 15.8km; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). If he still can't round the corner, the trace shows which arrows the probe tried and whether ANY progressed (if none, it's a true terrain pocket -> grid/route fix; if one did but he didn't follow, tune the window/align). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.76: #67 -- add a SLIDE-REJECTION guard to the screen-relative basis refresh. The .75 BAT was real progress: the refresh fix WORKED on open ground -- uHat/rHat tracked smoothly (they were frozen in .74) and he made the best on-foot progress yet, dist 16754->15807 (~950u) following the route east then starting north. But at the route corner he stalled against a blocked cell and then exactly the predicted wall-slide corruption hit: uHat rotated ~90deg from (-0.05,-1.00) to (1.00,-0.09) as the per-tick refresh learned from wall-slide/jitter motion (his real motion follows the wall, not the pressed arrow), and the corrupted basis then drove him BACKWARD -- dist climbed 15807->17404, farther out than he started. FIX: only refine the basis when the observed motion AGREES with the pressed arrow's predicted screen direction (dot > DRIVE_BASIS_AGREE_MIN=0.50, ~within 60deg) -- gentle camera tracking on open ground always agrees (~0.95) and passes; perpendicular/reversed wall-slide motion disagrees and is rejected, preserving the good basis. No other behavior change; threshold/Gram-Schmidt/calibration/decision/sidestep/vehicle path all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] uHat/rHat should track smoothly on open ground AND now HOLD steady (not swing ~90deg) when he contacts a wall, dist should keep dropping past 15.8km without the backward rout, and he should reach the route corner with a correct basis. Watch: (a) does uHat stay sane through the corner now; (b) does he still STALL at the corner (the .75 trace showed him nosing north into a blocked cell ~fine(104,69)=^ before the route's eastward leg -- if so the next fix is path-following/corner, NOT the basis) or does the sidestep round it; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.75: #67 -- FIX the screen-relative steering's dead basis-refresh (root cause of the .74 jam). The .74 BAT [SRDIAG] trace showed the approach WORKING at first (dist 15337->15028 while moving ~186u/frame) then wedging at the same ~15km coastal neck, and the smoking gun: uHat/rHat NEVER changed across the entire 15km drive (logged uHat(0.35,-0.93) rHat(0.93,0.36) on every line). The live basis-refresh was gated by DRIVE_BASIS_REFRESH_MIN=40, but per-tick walk motion is only ~15u (186u per 200ms / ~12 ticks), so refresh NEVER fired -- the basis stayed frozen at the drive-start camera angle while the world camera swung over 15km, so by the neck 'press UP' no longer meant north and steering drove him into the coast (keys=U--- forever, d+3-5, tgtBrg drifting). FIX: DRIVE_BASIS_REFRESH_MIN 40->10 so the basis tracks actual per-tick walking motion (still excludes wedge jitter <1u/tick); and after refreshing one axis from single-arrow motion, Gram-Schmidt the OTHER axis perpendicular to it (new OrthonormalizeAgainst) so the frame rotates rigidly with the camera and can't collapse (steering presses UP most of the time, so only uHat would refresh otherwise). No other behavior change; calibration, decision, sidestep, vehicle path all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] expect uHat/rHat to now MOVE smoothly as he drives (tracking the camera) and dist to keep dropping past 15km; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). Watch uHat in the trace: if it tracks smoothly and he progresses, the stale-basis bug is fixed; if instead uHat swings WILDLY when he slides along a wall, the next step is a slide-rejection guard (only refine when observed motion agrees with the basis). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.74: #67 -- REBUILD on-foot world-map steering as SCREEN-RELATIVE self-calibrating control (the real fix the .73 diagnostic pointed to). The .73 [CAMDIAG] BAT was decisive and NEGATIVE for the camera approach: injecting the manual's world-map camera keys (G=CCW, H=CW) did NOTHING -- across all 5 H taps and 5 G taps the hdng(0x0203ED00)/save(0x01CFFEB8) windows never changed and WM_HEADING stayed frozen at 3595; the only foot-window changes were the residual of the preceding UP-walk settling, not a per-tap camera ramp. So G/H don't rotate the camera in this build and there's no camera-angle field to read. BUT the scan confirmed the walk basis is clean and stable: UP-walk measured (-286,-286) three times (NW here), vs (+132,-132)=NE at the Dollet jam -- i.e. on foot the arrows WALK screen-relative and the screen->world mapping differs by region (the world camera faces different ways in different places), which is exactly why every heading-based steering attempt failed. FIX (foot drives only; vehicles keep the heading law): UpdateAutoDrive now MEASURES the screen->world basis from the character's own motion and presses the arrow combo whose screen direction points at the target. At drive start it calibrates -- press UP ~320ms -> world delta = screen-up unit vector uHat; settle; press RIGHT ~320ms -> screen-right unit vector rHat (retries up to 3x; perpendicular fallback if a probe can't move, corrected by live refresh) -- logged [SRDIAG] CAL. Then each tick it decomposes the wrapped world vector to the steer target onto (uHat,rHat): press UP if the screen-up component > cos(67.5deg), DOWN if < -that, RIGHT/LEFT likewise (8-way, diagonals allowed), and refreshes uHat/rHat live from any single-arrow motion (EMA) so the basis tracks the camera as it swings across the map. No heading, no pivot, no camera control, no reverse-into-loop. The on-foot stuck recovery is now a SIDESTEP (slide laterally past an obstacle, alternating sides) on the first stuck window, then the existing mid-route re-plan; vehicles keep the DOWN reverse burst. ALL routing/arrival/sweep/planner-eligibility scaffolding is UNCHANGED (the route was always fine; only the executor was broken). New state + constants in world_map_state.inl (DriveCalPhase, s_camU*/s_camR*, sidestep, DRIVE_CAL_*/STEER_AXIS_C/DRIVE_BASIS_*); helpers WrapWorldDelta + RefreshBasisAxis in world_map_drive.inl. Throttled [SRDIAG] trace logs pos/d+moved/tgtBrg/up+rt components/uHat/rHat/keys/SIDESTEP. BAT: on the world map on foot, select a far destination (Dollet) and press \ to auto-drive -- expect a brief UP-then-RIGHT calibration wobble at the start ([SRDIAG] CAL lines), then [SRDIAG] frames where up/rt pick the arrows toward the target and d+ stays POSITIVE (real progress, no eternal loop), and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- simple-coord, planner-ineligible, so only sidestep recovery). Read [SRDIAG]: if uHat/rHat look sane and the chosen arrows drive him toward tgtBrg, it's working; an inverted axis sign would show immediately as wrong arrows vs tgtBrg and is a one-line fix. The remaining #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.73: #67 -- DIAGNOSTIC build, no steering change yet. The v0.18.3.72 BAT trace was decisive: WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02) is FROZEN at 501 across the whole jam -- unchanged through PIVOT, STRAIGHT and REVERSE frames AND while he walked 600+ units in every direction during the DOWN bursts -- so it is NOT the on-foot facing, and the entire closed-loop heading-pivot steering (.66-.72) has been steering against a dead constant. On-foot world-map movement is DIRECTIONAL WALK (arrows walk screen-relative; measured from the trace: UP -> world (+132,-132), DOWN -> world (-132,+138), camera tilted ~45deg). UP walks him NE into the east coast -> pins; stuck-check fires DOWN -> walks him SW into open land; reverse ends -> UP walks him back to the same pin -> eternal loop. Reverse was never going to fix a steering loop that can't see its facing. Aaron's direction: have the mod OWN the camera (G/H rotate it -- confirmed via the FF8 PC manual: G=counter-clockwise, H=clockwise, NOT the Q/E first guessed) so it can both perceive AND control orientation. This build replaces the F12 diagnostic: RETIRED world_map_heading_scan.inl (HEADING_SCAN_DIAG 0 -- it injected RIGHT/LEFT, which are WALK keys on foot, so it rotated nothing and never found a facing) and added world_map_camera_scan.inl (CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG 1). F12 (dinput8.cpp) now calls WorldMap::TriggerCameraScan. The new scan, fully automated while the player holds still (~5s, TTS on start + completion, pause/resume around encounters): measures a short UP-walk vector (A), taps H x5 (clockwise) sampling 3 memory windows (foot 0x0203EE00 / hdng 0x0203ED00 / save 0x01CFFEB8) after each tap, measures UP-walk (B), taps G x5 (counter-clockwise), measures UP-walk (C). Reads from [CAMDIAG] in ff8_world.log: (1) the window offset whose b-delta ramps through the H taps and reverses through the G taps = the camera-angle field we can perceive; (2) whether the A/B/C UP-walk vectors rotate = movement is camera-relative; (3) per-tap rotation from B vs A. Decides the architecture: if G/H rotate the camera and a value tracks it, the real fix locks the camera to a canonical angle so UP always means a known world direction; else fall back to the empirically-derived walk basis with direct screen-relative arrow steering (like F9 field nav). BAT: on the world map standing still (NOT driving), press F12, hold still ~5s (you may hear a Gil readout from the G taps -- expected; if an encounter interrupts, fight it and it auto-restarts), then send Logs/ff8_world.log. No steering/routing behavior change this build. CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG + the remaining #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.72: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- make the reverse un-wedge ACTUALLY FIRE. The .71 BAT proved it still never fired, and the log showed exactly why: instead of a reverse burst, every mid-route stuck check fell through to '[DRIVE] Stuck mid-route -- re-planning ... recovery 5/8, 6/8' (a futile re-plan that recomputes the identical 21-cell route every time). Cause: the reverse had a separate 4-use budget (MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE) that got spent early in the drive and NEVER reset -- each random encounter (~every few seconds here) resumes him on the same neck and re-plans but does NOT reset the budget (only StartAutoDrive does, and the progress watermark only renews on getting closer, which never happens), so after 4 bursts the reverse was permanently locked out for the rest of the drive. FIX: drop the separate 4-use budget; fire the reverse on EVERY mid-route stuck check while there is still give-up headroom (s_driveStuckCount < DRIVE_STUCK_MAX), and let stuckCount keep climbing so it still gives up after DRIVE_STUCK_MAX if reversing never helps. stuckCount resets on resume, so each post-encounter segment gets a fresh set of attempts -- the reverse can no longer be starved. Still DOWN-only; the plan is DOWN backs him off the wall into open ground, then the normal PIVOT re-aims him (rotation appears to need open space -- turning in place while pinned does nothing, which is why the heading stays frozen at the wedge). NOTE: the route is confirmed FINE (the .71 [ROUTEMAP] again showed the single open corridor between ocean and blocked mountains). NOTE 2: random encounters are firing every few seconds at this spot, chopping the drive into short segments -- a separate concern, but segments are usually long enough for at least one 3s stuck check. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the jam expect '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst (check N/6)' then [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; the decisive reads are (1) does his pos/dist SHIFT on the REVERSE frames (DOWN moves him) and (2) once he's backed off, does hdg finally start CHANGING as the PIVOT re-aims him (rotation works in open space) -- if both, he should round the corner and continue. If reverse moves him but he still can't rotate after backing off, that's the next thing to chase. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.71: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REVERSE un-wedge, now actually triggered. Aaron confirmed pressing DOWN moves the character on the world map, so the reverse primitive is sound; the bug was that the recovery NEVER FIRED in the .68/.69/.70 BATs because it was keyed on a hard-wedge detector (no-movement in .68, net-displacement-250u in .69) that the wall-oscillation kept defeating. The .70 clearance change also had no effect on the route -- the [ROUTEMAP] showed it threading the ONE open north-south corridor between the ocean (east) and a band of blocked mountain cells (west); col 105 is already the clearance-optimal lane, so a stronger bias had nothing better to move to. Routing is fine; the blocker is purely the start-cell wedge (he resumes on a narrow water/cliff neck, hdg frozen 254, can't turn out -- a full second of LEFT-only never moved the heading -- and 'forward' slides him along the waterline). FIX: trigger the reverse burst from the PROVEN stuck-check (it logs 'Stuck check N/6' reliably every 3s) instead of the dead hard-wedge path, and make the burst DOWN-only (no simultaneous turn -- turning while wedged does nothing and muddied .68/.69; a clean backward push into open ground, then normal steering re-aims). On a mid-route stuck check (dist>=DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST, budget MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE left) it sets s_driveWedgeReverseUntil=now+REVERSE_BURST_MS, logs '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4', resets the give-up counter, and returns so DOWN is pressed from the next tick; the progress watermark renews the budget once a reverse gets him >=WEDGE_PROGRESS_EPS closer, else it falls through to re-plan/give-up. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the jam expect '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst 1/4' then [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; the decisive read is whether d+ goes POSITIVE and his position/dist actually shifts on those frames (reverse frees him -> deadlock broken, tune from there) or stays ~0 (something still blocks it). If he backs off and then re-aims and continues, that's the win. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.70: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- CLEARANCE-PREFERRING PLANNER (Aaron-requested): prioritize an open, clean path over minimizing distance. The .69 BAT (wedge detector still didn't fire -- this run the oscillation amplitude was ~840u E-W, far above WEDGE_NET_EPS=250, so the net-displacement anchor kept resetting) came with F11 screenshots that finally showed the real problem: the player is sawing east-west along a thin SHORELINE strip at the water's edge with a WIDE-OPEN grass field right beside him -- the route hugs the coast when open ground is adjacent, and 'forward' just slides him along the waterline (hdg frozen 7, Y pinned ~-26360, X swinging 840u, dist stuck ~15km). That is a routing problem, not a wedge-recovery problem: the route should go through the open grass, not along the water. PlanPathFine already biases toward clearance (s_clearFine = Chebyshev dist to nearest ocean/steep-mountain), but far too weakly (WM_CLEAR_PENALTY=6, WM_CLEAR_TARGET=3 made a wall/water-adjacent cell cost only 13 vs 1 open). Raised to WM_CLEAR_PENALTY=20, WM_CLEAR_TARGET=5: a clearance-1 (edge-hugging) cell now costs ~81 vs 1 for open ground (clearance>=5), so the Dijkstra detours FAR to stay clear of water/mountain edges and only dips to low clearance at the unavoidable coastal destination itself. Reachability is unchanged (costs stay finite; IsFineTraversable untouched) -- only the cost weighting changed, so a genuinely narrow-only corridor is still taken, just at high cost. Tunable; the [ROUTEMAP] dump at plan time shows the new route immediately. BAT: drive to Dollet -- check [ROUTEMAP] in ff8_world.log shows the route now running through OPEN ground (inland) rather than along the coast, and the player should stay on open grass where the steering already works (it drove 19->17km smoothly on open ground in .66) and ARRIVE; ALSO a Balamb drive (regression -- the stronger bias changes every route). If he still pins on the new open route, next step is the progress-based wedge detector (the .68/.69 reverse recovery never got to fire -- DOWN is still untested). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.69: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- wedge detector FIX: key the hard-wedge on NET displacement, not raw movement. The .68 BAT proved the reverse un-wedge NEVER fired -- no [DRIVE] burst line, no D key the whole run -- because the jam is a wall-VIBRATION, not stillness: the player bounces ~60u east-west every frame (per-frame d+ 30-62) while netting ~zero forward progress (dist pinned at 14960), and those bounces kept resetting the .68 move-timer so the hard-wedge (no-movement for HARD_WEDGE_MS) never tripped. The mod's own slow stuck-check DID see it (moved 6/9 units in 3000ms) but that only feeds give-up. Fix: detect the wedge by NET displacement from an anchor that only resets when he genuinely relocates WEDGE_NET_EPS=250u, so vibration-in-place trips hardWedge after HARD_WEDGE_MS and the reverse finally fires. Secondary finding (informs .70): pressing LEFT to turn while MOVING 60+ units did NOT rotate him (hdg frozen at 201 all run), so turning is dead while jammed -- relocating via reverse is the needed escape, and this BAT is the decisive test of whether DOWN does anything on the world map. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the corner expect [DRIVE] 'Hard wedge -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4' + [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; READ whether d+ (and especially the net pos change) goes POSITIVE on those frames. If yes, reverse works -> tune from there. If d+~0 on REVERSE too, DOWN is inert on the world map -> .70 attacks it from routing. Next (.70, Aaron-requested): clearance-preferring planner -- bias the route toward open ground over shortest distance so it avoids pinch corners in the first place. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.68: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REVERSE un-wedge recovery. The .67 route-map dump answered Aaron's routing question: the route is directionally correct (NE to Dollet) but runs along the coast for the north leg (the inland is mountain-broken in the grid), and the recurring 15km jam is at the east->north CORNER against the coast. The trace pinned the mechanism: on the world map the character only ROTATES while moving forward, so when he noses into the coast at the corner he can't move, can't turn, and his heading FREEZES (hdg stuck at 501 the whole jam) -- he just presses forward into the same water forever. The closed-loop steering itself works (drove 19km->17km smoothly on the open leg before a random encounter); what was missing is wedge recovery. Fix: add a DOWN-arrow (reverse) injection + a wedge-recovery branch in UpdateAutoDrive. On a HARD wedge (no real movement for HARD_WEDGE_MS=1200, longer than the WALL_SLIDE_MS slide window), reverse off the wall for REVERSE_BURST_MS=600 while turning toward the target (back-and-aim) to regain open ground + motion, then resume normal steering. Bounded to MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE=4 bursts per wedge; the budget renews only on genuine progress (got WEDGE_PROGRESS_EPS=500u closer), so a true no-progress jam still falls through to the existing stuck-detection / re-plan / give-up instead of reversing forever. Also dropped the counterproductive wall-jam forced-RIGHT nudge (it shoved him away from a left-turn target). Key injection: SetDriveKeys gains a 4th 'down' param (VK_DOWN 0x28 / scan 0x50, extended); ReleaseAllDriveKeys releases it. [HDG-DIAG] gains a REVERSE band + a D key column. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- at the coastal corner expect [DRIVE] 'Hard wedge -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4' lines and [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames where d+ goes POSITIVE (he backs off) and hdg starts changing again, then him rounding the corner and continuing. If reverse moves him (d+>0 on REVERSE frames) the deadlock is broken even if tuning is needed; if d+ stays ~0 on REVERSE too, DOWN does nothing on the world map and we need a different un-wedge. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG / ROUTE_MAP_DIAG / HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.67: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- DIAGNOSTIC: route-map dump to verify the planner actually picks the right path to Dollet. The .66 closed-loop steering BAT jammed ~15km out on a coastal spit (F11 screenshot: tall cliff NW, ocean E, narrow shoreline strip), and [HDG-DIAG] showed hdg FROZEN at 502 the whole time while keys cycled U/L/R -- he could not rotate out. But the deeper question (Aaron) is whether the ROUTE is even correct: you reach Dollet via an inland canyon, not by hugging the coast, and the log only prints the route's endpoints (21 cells fine(105,70)->fine(112,57)), never the intermediate cells, so neither of us can tell if the path threads the canyon or scrapes the shore. New DumpRouteMap (gated ROUTE_MAP_DIAG, called in PlanPathFine at plan time) logs an ASCII map of the REAL fine terrain grid spanning the planned route with the route overlaid (.=land f=forest m=gentle-mtn ~=ocean; CAPS/X/^=blocked for this vehicle; S=start D=Dollet G=goal o=route; row 0=north, col 0=west), so we can SEE whether the chosen path is the canyon or a coast-hug AND whether the grid's walkable/blocked classification is even right. No steering/routing behavior change. BAT: start a Dollet auto-drive -- the [ROUTEMAP] block logs IMMEDIATELY at plan time, so no need to drive into the jam; just send Logs/ff8_world.log. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG (plus DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + HEADING_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.66: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REBUILD steering as closed-loop control on the REAL facing. The .65 F12 heading scan was decisive: WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02, exactly what GetWorldMapHeading already reads) IS the live facing -- holding RIGHT raised it ~80/sample (3595->4051->wrap->99, clockwise), holding LEFT reversed it, in the SAME compass units as TorusBearing (0=N, clockwise). The earlier 'frozen' DIAGNOSIS was wrong in its conclusion: the value wasn't dead, the character simply wasn't rotating during those drives -- he was jammed pressing forward+turn into terrain, which the engine won't rotate, so his facing genuinely stayed at his resting value (3595 in .62-.64, 540 in .61 = just each save's start facing). The motion-derived workaround then layered wall-slide corruption on top. Fix: UpdateAutoDrive now steers closed-loop on GetWorldMapHeading with a TURN-THEN-GO law -- err = targetBearing - heading (wrapped to [-2048,2048]); when |err| > STEER_DEADZONE, PIVOT turn-only (RIGHT if err>=0 since RIGHT raises the heading, else LEFT; NO forward, so the engine rotates him freely and the heading updates -- exactly the scan's clean turn-only phase); once aligned, drive forward; within FINAL_APPROACH_FORWARD_DIST just walk straight; a wall-jam watchdog forces a pivot if he stops moving while aimed. A true heading lets the pivot stop the instant we're aligned (kills the oscillation), and turn-only sidesteps the arc-into-wall jam that locked rotation before. ALL routing (clearance Dijkstra + path lookahead) is reused UNCHANGED -- this is the steering law only, general, not per-town. The motion-derived heading machinery (s_driveMoveHeading + runtime turn-sign calibration + duty-cycle) is gone from the steering path. New throttled [HDG-DIAG] trace logs the real heading vs target bearing + err + keys to confirm it tracks during a live drive. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] hdg to rotate toward tgtBrg while PIVOTing, err to shrink and stay small on STRAIGHT frames, and him to thread the canyon and ARRIVE; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG and the F12 heading scan (HEADING_SCAN_DIAG) both retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.65: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- STEP BACK from steering tuning to a live-facing DISCOVERY diagnostic. Ten builds of steering work all fought one missing sensor: we cannot read which way the character is FACING. WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02) is frozen -- the .61 diagnostic proved it returns a constant all session -- so every steering decision since has run on a motion-derived heading that lags the turn and is corrupted whenever he slides along a wall (his motion then follows the wall, not his facing): the source of the .63/.64 oscillation-then-wedge. The real facing DOES exist in memory (FFNx's world.cpp reads the live camera out of sub_4023D0; the savemap WORLDMAP struct carries a rotation field) -- we just have the wrong address. New world_map_heading_scan.inl finds it deterministically and blind-accessibly: on the world map, press F12 -- while the player holds still it INJECTS a known turn (RIGHT ~1.6s then LEFT ~1.6s, turn-only via SetDriveKeys(0,0,1)/(0,1,0) so he pivots roughly in place, no forward/gas) and dumps three candidate memory windows (foot@0x0203EE00 256B, hdng@0x0203ED00 64B, save@0x01CFFEB8 32B) interpreted as int16 words every 150ms, logging each changed word's per-sample (s) and per-baseline (b) delta under [HSCAN]. The live facing is the offset whose s-delta holds ONE sign across the RIGHT samples and FLIPS across the LEFT samples (frame counters ramp the same sign both phases; position words move only if he translated). Announces \"Heading scan complete\" on finish; pauses and resumes around random encounters via Poll()'s existing world-map exit/entry detection (HScanPause/HScanResume -- the same mechanism auto-drive uses), re-snapshotting the baseline and restarting the interrupted turn phase on resume. F12 wired in dinput8.cpp gated on !alt (it was the free per-session diagnostic key; no old F12 handler to retire). No steering/routing change this build. BAT: on the world map standing still, press F12, let it run ~4s (if an encounter interrupts, fight it -- the scan announces \"paused\" then \"resuming\" and continues), then send Logs/ff8_world.log; the [HSCAN] block reveals the live facing address, which the next build rebuilds steering around as proper closed-loop control (no motion-derivation, no wall-corruption, no oscillation). All routing is reused as-is; this is NOT route-hardcoding. // v0.18.3.64: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- damp the steering oscillation + tighten the lookahead. The .63 BAT was real progress: sign=+1 held, the player drove the RIGHT direction and covered ~3km toward Dollet (19km->16km), then PINNED at a tight canyon bend, oscillating in place -- [DRIVE-DIAG] showed his motion heading swinging ~180deg past the target and back every frame (mh 2749<->746, keys U-R<->UL-), d+ ~2-6, dist stuck at 16446, until the stuck-check fired. Two causes: (1) bang-bang turning with no damping -- holding the turn key every frame over-rotates ~180deg past the target, then reverses and overshoots back; (2) the 3-cell lookahead again aimed at a point across the canyon wall, so he mashed into it and his motion-derived heading read the wall-deflected motion. Two fixes: aim at the NEXT path cell only (DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS 3->1) -- consecutive BFS path cells are 4-adjacent and walkable, so the straight line to the target can never cross a wall; and DUTY-CYCLE the turn (TURN_DUTY_ON_MS 60 / OFF 120, ~33%) so straight forward frames between turn pulses let the heading catch up and he converges instead of spinning. Full turn still applies while wall-jammed. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] err to actually SHRINK and stay small (not swing +1600/-450), d+ to stay positive (forward progress), dist to keep dropping past 16km to arrival; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.63: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 follow-up to the .62 root-cause fix. The .62 BAT was the breakthrough -- the motion-derived heading made the player MOBILE for the first time (he traversed the continent; mh tracked his motion while the raw read stayed frozen at a constant 3595) -- but he drove to Galbadia Garden instead of Dollet. The [DRIVE-DIAG] trace showed two faults. (1) The runtime turn-sign calibration FLIPPED THE SIGN WRONG: it learns the sign by watching the derived heading rotate when turning, but in the canyon he slides along walls where movement direction follows the WALL not his facing, so the calibration read garbage; with sign=-1 the steering pressed LEFT for a right-side target, curving him away from Dollet. (2) At the start he was aimed almost dead-on at the target (err=+56) but JAMMED against the canyon wall, and the far 2400u straight-line lookahead pointed across that wall, so the wall-slide spun him ~180deg the wrong way before he broke free and committed to the wrong direction. Two fixes: the turn sign is now FIXED at +1 (TorusBearing is compass-style -- north 0, clockwise, confirmed by tgtBrg=477 for a NE target -- so pressing RIGHT turns clockwise = raises the bearing) and the wall-corrupted runtime calibration is removed; and the steering target is now DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS(3) cells AHEAD ALONG THE ROUTE instead of the first cell >=2400u in a straight line, so the aim stays inside the winding corridor rather than cutting across a wall. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] sign=+1 throughout, err to shrink as he follows the canyon, and him to head down the canyon toward Dollet rather than toward Galbadia Garden; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.62: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 ROOT-CAUSE FIX -- steer by a motion-derived heading instead of the frozen GetWorldMapHeading(). The v0.18.3.61 diagnostic proved the heading read returns a constant 540 for the ENTIRE drive (head=540 dH=+0 on every line) while position and keys varied, so every relBearing across all six prior BATs was computed against a fake facing and the steering turned the WRONG way -- it curved the player west (away from eastward Dollet) into the coastline and jammed pressing UP into collision (d+4 for ~5s). Fix: ignore GetWorldMapHeading entirely for steering and derive the facing from the bearing of the player's recent position delta (his real movement direction is exactly what steering needs), re-sampled every MOVE_HEADING_MIN_DELTA(50) units. The one unknown -- does RIGHT raise or lower that bearing -- is LEARNED at runtime: when a turn rotates the derived heading by >TURN_CAL_MIN_DELTA(80), s_driveTurnSign is set from the observed direction, so a wrong initial guess self-corrects within a sample and cannot steer wrong-way for long. Always press forward (no pivot-in-place) so motion -- and thus the heading + calibration -- never stalls; plus a wall-slide (force a turn after WALL_SLIDE_MS(600) with no movement) to nose off terrain. Replaces BOTH the mid-route and final-approach steering branches (both fed off the broken heading). [DRIVE-DIAG] now logs mh(derived) vs raw(broken) heading + signed err + learned sign. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] mh to TRACK his motion (not stuck at 540), err to shrink, and him to actually move toward Dollet; also BAT a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.61: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 DIAGNOSTIC build (no behavior change beyond logging). Six BATs of the on-foot Dollet auto-drive have failed, each diagnosed from sparse data (a 'moved N units in 3000ms' stuck-check every 3s plus one F11), and the fixes have not converged because the actual per-tick steering behavior is not visible -- the v0.18.3.60 BAT left the player essentially stationary (16 units / 3s) near the coastal start, recovery-replanning to 6/8 before a random encounter, and the logs cannot distinguish pivot-in-place (turning, never committing forward) from forward-into-collision (UP pressed but blocked by walkmesh geometry the 1024u grid cannot see) -- which need opposite fixes. This build adds a throttled [DRIVE-DIAG] trace (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG, ~5 lines/sec) in UpdateAutoDrive logging position + delta-moved, heading + delta, target bearing, relBearing/off, keys pressed (U/L/R), and the band taken (STRAIGHT/ARC/PIVOT/final). The per-line delta-moved tells the three cases apart: PIVOT band with dH changing + d+~0 = pivoting in place; STRAIGHT/ARC with U pressed but d+~0 = walking into collision; d+ large = real progress. No steering/routing change. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet, let it sit stuck ~10s, then send ff8_world.log (the [DRIVE-DIAG] block) + an F11 -- the trace will show exactly why he is not moving, and the next build is a targeted fix. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG must be set false (or the trace removed) before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.60: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 4 -- mid-route steering law: dead-zone + arc + clean wrap, to kill the heading-overshoot oscillation. The v0.18.3.59 BAT routed correctly (clearance-weighted 23 cells to Dollet fine(112,57)) but the drive OSCILLATED in place at the start: the player rocked across the cell-103/104 boundary (~33u E-W), creeping north only a few units, then a random encounter (MODE_SWIRL) interrupted; F11 = on foot near the coast (loco byte noise=35). Diagnosed offline against the real route -- NOT the routing or lookahead (the lookahead pointed the same way, NE ~45deg off north, from both cells he rocked between); the steering law itself. The old law walked forward (UP) only within ~17.5deg of the target and turned in place otherwise, at a fixed rate with no damping, so the heading overshot the target and rocked across it while rarely engaging forward -- weaving in place instead of driving. Across five prior BATs the routing failures (.55/.56) are fixed and the follow failures (.57 drift, .58 crawl, .59 oscillation) all trace to this one discrete turn-law. Fix: replace ONLY the mid-route branch (dist >= DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST) with an arc law -- fold relBearing into an absolute offset + side (no hard L/R boundary to rock on), then within STEER_DEADZONE (~28deg) drive STRAIGHT with no turn (damps the overshoot -- stop correcting once roughly aimed); within STEER_FWD_CONE (~50deg) drive forward AND turn (arc); beyond it pivot in place toward the target (sharp bends, as before). Final-approach branch UNCHANGED. STEER_DEADZONE/STEER_FWD_CONE are tunable (widen deadzone if it still oscillates, narrow if it drifts into walls). This changes EVERY drive's long-distance steering, so BAT must ALSO regression-check a known-good Balamb drive. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet (thread the canyon + ARRIVE; [DRIVE] re-planning lines = recovery not failure) AND a Balamb destination (no regression). // v0.18.3.59: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 3 -- clearance-centred routing + min-distance lookahead + stuck recovery (pre-plan the route, then drive smoothly along it). Four BATs of the fine planner each peeled a distinct bug; the last two exposed a real tension -- a far lookahead DRIFTS off the path into a wall (v0.18.3.57) while a one-cell lookahead CRAWLS (v0.18.3.58): the steering law walks forward only when the target is within ~45deg of heading and turns in place otherwise, and a 1024u target swings the bearing as the player inches, so it spins. Offline analysis showed the walkable route to Dollet is a genuinely narrow winding canyon (~1-2 fine cells wide; even a clearance-centred route averages only 1.7 cells of wall clearance), so the grid's 1024u approximation of the real walkmesh is least accurate exactly there. Three coordinated changes: (1) PlanPathFine is now a clearance-weighted Dijkstra -- s_clearFine (Chebyshev distance to the nearest blocked cell, computed once at grid-load by multi-source BFS) makes a step into a low-clearance cell cost 1 + WM_CLEAR_PENALTY*(WM_CLEAR_TARGET-clearance), so the route threads the corridor CENTRE instead of scraping walls; (2) the drive steers at the first path cell >= DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST (2400u) ahead -- far enough for a stable bearing (no crawl), close enough that the straight-line cut stays inside the corridor (no drift); (3) mid-route stuck recovery re-plans from the player's CURRENT position (a fresh clearance route from where he actually is) up to DRIVE_MAX_REPLANS times before giving up. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: the clearance Dijkstra routes 30 cells to Dollet fine(112,57) (avg clearance 1.70, reaches the target); the offline follow-sim with the real steering law arrives with ~720u deviation at this lookahead. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- expect [WALKFINE] ...clearance max/wall-hugging..., [PLAN] Fine path ...clearance-weighted, and the player to thread the canyon and ARRIVE; any [DRIVE] re-planning lines are recovery, not failure. // v0.18.3.58: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 2 -- steer to the NEXT path cell, not a far line-of-sight lookahead. The v0.18.3.57 BAT routed correctly (27 fine cells around the cliff to Dollet) but the drive still failed ~12km out: the LOS lookahead (farthest path cell with a clear cell-centre straight line, up to 8 ahead) steered the player diagonally OFF the straight-north path corridor -- the 1024-unit cell-centre Bresenham looked clear, but the game's finer cliff geometry between cell centres blocked the diagonal, so the player drifted ~3 cells east and jammed against a cliff (stuck at fine(103,64) with the cliff cell at fine(103,63) directly north). A long lookahead cuts corners; that recurred across BATs. Fix: the drive now steers to s_drivePath[idx+1] only -- consecutive path cells are 4-adjacent and walkable, so the player can't cut a corner or drift off-corridor, and the cursor's per-tick re-projection to the nearest path cell steers him back on if a collision nudges him off. Trades steering smoothness for faithfully hugging the route (FineLineWalkable now unused). BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- the player should follow the path corridor around the cliff and ARRIVE. // v0.18.3.57: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX -- route to the destination coordinate, not the goal region. The v0.18.3.56 BAT failed to reach Dollet: PlanPathFine routed to the nearest cell of the goal REGION, but Dollet's region 0x01 is large and reaches right next to the player, so the fine path was only 3 cells north and the drive then steered NE into the same cliff. Fix: PlanPathFine now floods the reachable component from the player and routes to the visited fine cell NEAREST the destination coordinate (s_driveTargetX/Y) -- the target itself when reachable, which the catalog already vetted. s_driveGoalSegs is retained for arrival detection only. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: Dollet now routes 20 fine cells AROUND the cliff to the actual target fine(112,57) (dist 0), zero blocked cells on the path. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- expect ff8_world.log '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells ... nearest reachable to target' and arrival, not a hang. // v0.18.3.56: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- point the auto-drive planner at the fine slope-aware grid. The coarse 32x24 A* (PlanPath over s_terrainGrid, segment-centre waypoints) routed straight lines through mountains it couldn't see -- the v0.18.3.55 BAT hung the Dollet drive at the base of a steep cliff because the planner laid a waypoint due north into terrain the fine grid already knew was impassable. PlanPathFine replaces it: a 4-connected torus BFS over the 256x192 fine grid using the SAME IsFineTraversable rule as catalog reachability (s_walkClassFine + s_steepFine), to the nearest walkable fine cell in the goal region (s_driveGoalSegs), storing the route as packed fine cells in s_drivePath. The drive follows it with a line-of-sight lookahead (FineLineWalkable Bresenham, up to 8 cells): it steers toward the farthest path cell reachable in a clear walkable straight line, smoothing diagonal staircases AND guaranteeing it never aims the player across a blocked cell (a fixed lookahead would cut the cliff corner). On final approach / path end it steers to the real destination coord. Region/goal selection (MatchProgramForCatalog/CollectGoalSegments) is unchanged -- only the routing grid changed; geometric-trigger destinations (Fire Cavern, early Balamb Garden) still use simple-coord steering and are unaffected. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: the Dollet route is 20 fine cells AROUND the cliff (fine(101,65)->fine(112,57), zero blocked cells); LOS lookahead crosses no blocked cell at any cursor. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet and to Galbadia Garden -- ff8_world.log should show '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells', and the drive should route around mountains and ARRIVE instead of hanging. // v0.18.3.55: #67 BAT 2 stage 1 -- slope-aware fine grid (mountains). The fine reachability grid now carries a per-cell steepness (the elevation spread of the wmx polygon at the cell, read from the vertex elevation field previously discarded during the wmx load). IsFineTraversable blocks a MOUNTAIN-class cell whose steepness exceeds WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK (256) for foot/chocobo/car -- steep mountain faces are impassable; gentle mountain cells (passes/plateaus) stay walkable, so the map doesn't over-fragment (blanket type-29 blocking isolated Esthar and split Edea from Centra -- real passes exist). Garden/Ragnarok ignore it (hover/fly). Threshold calibrated offline against destination reachability: at 256, every Galbadia and Balamb catalog destination stays reachable AND the full reachable set is unchanged vs no blocking; it is refined in-game via the [WM-CALIB] steepness trace (now logs steep=N plus a BLOCKED flag per cell). Empirical terrain re-eval also confirmed the six undocumented terrain types present (17/18/23/24/25/31) all sit at land elevations and are gentle -- genuine walkable land, NOT hidden water; all map water is the ocean class already blocked. This stage touches reachability/catalog only; the AD planner still routes on the coarse grid (stage 2 points it at the fine grid). BAT: load a Galbadia save then a Balamb save and cycle the catalog -- expect the SAME destinations as v0.18.3.54 (none lost); check ff8_world.log [WALKFINE] (mountain steep-blocked count) and [WM-CALIB] (it should never log the player standing on a BLOCKED cell -- if it does, the threshold is too low).
